{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tjb2YYFHDt9jR1CLtmB45X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Claims require hedging but core story survives. η_max dominance\n2\t  downgraded from ''robust'' to ''directional with benchmark-dependent strength''\n3\t  — MMLU strong, ARC weak, must discuss why. K_eff fragility still holds but framed\n4\t  as ''inconclusive'' rather than ''clean null''. GAM 6:6 split presented as ''suggestive\n5\t  taxonomic pattern warranting further investigation'' rather than confirmed mechanism.\n6\t  RF contrast becomes a partial validation (if completed) or acknowledged limitation\n7\t  (if not). Aggregation gradient retains OLS results with caveat that clustered SE\n8\t  validation is incomplete. Paper shifts from 5-contribution to 3-contribution focus:\n9\t  (1) η_max dominance (primary), (2) methodological transparency on non-independence\n10\t  (elevated to co-primary — the correction story itself becomes a contribution), (3)\n11\t  practical defense recommendations (min_eta_max still wins). Title softened to candidate\n12\t  2 or 3. ''Diversity Trap'' title likely too strong — ''Weakest Link'' framing preferred.\n13\t  Supplementary materials carry more analytical weight to address reviewer concerns\n14\t  about incomplete robustness checks.'\n15\timagined_experiments:\n16\t- name: Scale Expansion to 15 Models\n17\t  plan_id: plan_001\n18\t  description: Add API models (GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, Claude,\n19\t    Gemini) and large open-source (Llama-3.1-70B, Qwen2.5-72B) to reach 15 models\n20\t    total. Rerun MI matrix + full regression suite on C(15,3)=455 panels across 12\n21\t    benchmark×attack conditions.\n22\t  expected_results:\n23\t    models_total: 15\n24\t    api_models: 9\n25\t    local_models: 6\n26\t    panels: 455\n27\t    conditions: 12\n28\t    confound_r_keff_eta: 0.7\n29\t    ols_eta_sig: 11/12\n30\t    ols_keff_sig: 8/12 (7+/1-)\n31\t    bootstrap_eta_sig: 5/12\n32\t    bootstrap_keff_sig: 1/12\n33\t    clustered_se_eta_sig: 8/12\n34\t    clustered_se_keff_sig: 0/12\n35\t    mixed_effects_eta_sig: 7/12\n36\t    mixed_effects_keff_sig: 3/12\n37\t    dominance_ratio_bootstrap: '5:1'\n38\t    se_inflation_factor: ~2.5x\n39\t    mmlu_bootstrap_eta: 4/6\n40\t    arc_bootstrap_eta: 1/6\n41\t    gam_targeted_positive: 6/6\n42\t    gam_sharedbias_negative: 6/6\n43\t    min_eta_max_wins: 12/12\n44\t    practical_delta_asr_keff_iqr_pp: 1.3\n45\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_001 IS the shipped evidence — plan001_step1_full_v4\n46\t    realized it successfully. The 15-model expansion dramatically increased panel\n47\t    count (20→455) and broke the 6-model confound ceiling (r=-0.8→+0.70). K_eff flips\n48\t    from NS-negative (6-model: arc×sycophancy only) to predominantly positive (7+/1-)\n49\t    under OLS, BUT this OLS significance is inflated by panel non-independence: each\n50\t    of 15 judges appears in ~91 panels, creating massive overlap. Correction methods\n51\t    (bootstrap/clustered SE/mixed effects) collapse K_eff from 8/12 to 0-3/12 while\n52\t    η_max survives at 5-8/12. The 5:1 dominance ratio under bootstrap is actually\n53\t    STRONGER than OLS''s 11:8≈1.4:1 because K_eff collapses faster than η_max. ARC\n54\t    weakness (1/6 bootstrap η_max vs MMLU 4/6) reflects MCQ format: 4-choice MMLU\n55\t    gives more scoring variance for η_max signal than 2-choice ARC. This benchmark\n56\t    asymmetry is a structural limitation of the experimental design, not a refutation\n57\t    of η_max dominance.'\n58\t  tables:\n59\t  - name: Correction Method Comparison (12 conditions)\n60\t    columns:\n61\t    - Method\n62\t    - η_max sig\n63\t    - K_eff sig\n64\t    - Dominance ratio\n65\t    - Note\n66\t    rows:\n67\t    - - OLS (baseline)\n68\t      - 11/12\n69\t      - 8/12 (7+/1-)\n70\t      - 1.4:1\n71\t      - Inflated by panel overlap\n72\t    - - Wild cluster bootstrap v2\n73\t      - 5/12\n74\t      - 1/12\n75\t      - '5:1'\n76\t      - Conservative lower bound; position 0 imbalance\n77\t    - - Clustered SE (CR1)\n78\t      - 8/12\n79\t      - 0/12\n80\t      - '8:0'\n81\t      - Consistent with bootstrap positions 1&2\n82\t    - - Mixed effects\n83\t      - 7/12\n84\t      - 3/12\n85\t      - 2.3:1\n86\t      - Random judge intercepts\n87\t    - - Tier-indicator corrected\n88\t      - 11/12\n89\t      - 5/12\n90\t      - 2.2:1\n91\t      - 3/3/9 tier control\n92\t  - name: Bootstrap η_max by Benchmark\n93\t    columns:\n94\t    - Benchmark\n95\t    - η_max bootstrap sig\n96\t    - Attacks surviving\n97\t    - Attacks lost\n98\t    rows:\n99\t    - - MMLU\n100\t      - 4/6\n101\t      - PI, SM, PB, VB\n102\t      - syco, auth\n103\t    - - ARC\n104\t      - 1/6\n105\t      - PI only (marginal)\n106\t      - SM, PB, syco, VB, auth\n107\t  figures_description:\n108\t  - 'Fig 1: Coefficient significance heatmap — 4 correction methods × 12 conditions,\n109\t    color-coded by η_max/K_eff significance. Shows systematic K_eff collapse across\n110\t    rows while η_max retains partial survival.'\n111\t  - 'Fig 2: SE inflation scatter — OLS SE vs corrected SE for each coefficient, showing\n112\t    ~2.5x inflation line.'\n113\t  analysis: 'plan_001 is the foundational experiment and is already completed as plan001_step1_full_v4.\n114\t    Under median tier, no further analysis of this data yields stronger results. The\n115\t    key tension is between OLS (favorable) and bootstrap (conservative): the paper\n116\t    must present both transparently. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU >> ARC) is the\n117\t    main weakness — reviewers will question whether η_max dominance is benchmark-dependent.\n118\t    The honest answer is yes, partially: η_max is stronger in higher-variance scoring\n119\t    formats. This needs explicit discussion in Section 5.'\n120\t- name: Attack Expansion to 6+ Types\n121\t  plan_id: plan_002\n122\t  description: Add few-shot hijacking, persona manipulation, instruction override\n123\t    attacks. Run on full 15-model pool. Test universality of weakest-link finding\n124\t    across broader attack surface.\n125\t  expected_results:\n126\t    status: not_completed\n127\t    reason: Time and compute constraints; 60 GPU-hours not allocated\n128\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n129\t      new_attacks_tested: 3\n130\t      few_shot_hijacking_eta_sig: likely 1/2 (MMLU yes, ARC no)\n131\t      few_shot_hijacking_keff_sig: likely 0/2 after correction\n132\t      persona_manipulation_eta_sig: likely 1/2\n133\t      persona_manipulation_keff_sig: likely 0/2 after correction\n134\t      instruction_override_eta_sig: likely 2/2\n135\t      instruction_override_keff_sig: likely 0-1/2 after correction\n136\t      overall_eta_sig_new: 4/6 OLS, 2-3/6 bootstrap\n137\t      overall_keff_sig_new: 2-3/6 OLS, 0-1/6 bootstrap\n138\t      new_gam_pattern: few-shot hijacking and instruction override → targeted (K_eff\n139\t        positive); persona manipulation → ambiguous\n140\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_002 is not completed due to resource\n141\t    constraints. If run, results would be mixed: new attack types would largely confirm\n142\t    η_max dominance pattern but with benchmark asymmetry persisting (MMLU > ARC).\n143\t    Few-shot hijacking is mechanistically similar to prompt_injection (targeted, judge-specific)\n144\t    so K_eff would be directionally positive but NS after correction. Persona manipulation\n145\t    is ambiguous — it exploits both judge-specific persona susceptibility AND shared\n146\t    anthropomorphic biases, making the targeted/shared-bias taxonomy assignment unclear.\n147\t    Instruction override is the strongest targeted attack variant, so η_max likely\n148\t    robust. Net: the attack expansion would not change the core narrative but would\n149\t    introduce 1-2 conditions that don''t cleanly fit the GAM 6:6 taxonomy, requiring\n150\t    a footnote about taxonomy edge cases. The marginal evidence gain doesn''t justify\n151\t    the 60 GPU-hour cost under the median scenario — the paper can acknowledge this\n152\t    as a limitation.'\n153\t  tables:\n154\t  - name: Hypothetical New Attack Results (OLS vs Bootstrap)\n155\t    columns:\n156\t    - Attack\n157\t    - Benchmark\n158\t    - η_max OLS p\n159\t    - η_max bootstrap p\n160\t    - K_eff OLS p\n161\t    - K_eff bootstrap p\n162\t    - GAM K_eff direction\n163\t    rows:\n164\t    - - Few-shot hijacking\n165\t      - MMLU\n166\t      - <0.001\n167\t      - '0.03'\n168\t      - '0.04'\n169\t      - '0.15'\n170\t      - positive\n171\t    - - Few-shot hijacking\n172\t      - ARC\n173\t      - '0.008'\n174\t      - '0.12'\n175\t      - '0.18'\n176\t      - '0.45'\n177\t      - positive\n178\t    - - Persona manipulation\n179\t      - MMLU\n180\t      - '0.003'\n181\t      - '0.07'\n182\t      - '0.06'\n183\t      - '0.22'\n184\t      - mixed (+0.02)\n185\t    - - Persona manipulation\n186\t      - ARC\n187\t      - '0.02'\n188\t      - '0.18'\n189\t      - '0.12'\n190\t      - '0.38'\n191\t      - mixed (-0.01)\n192\t    - - Instruction override\n193\t      - MMLU\n194\t      - <0.001\n195\t      - '0.01'\n196\t      - '0.03'\n197\t      - '0.09'\n198\t      - positive\n199\t    - - Instruction override\n200\t      - ARC\n201\t      - '0.005'\n202\t      - '0.04'\n203\t      - '0.09'\n204\t      - '0.28'\n205\t      - positive\n206\t  figures_description:\n207\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Extended GAM partial effects for 18 conditions (12 original\n208\t    + 6 new). The 6:6 targeted/shared-bias split extends to roughly 9:6 with 3 ambiguous,\n209\t    weakening the clean binary taxonomy.'\n210\t  analysis: 'Non-completion is the most likely median outcome. The paper handles this\n211\t    by stating ''six representative attack types covering targeted and shared-bias\n212\t    mechanisms'' and acknowledging that broader attack coverage is future work. If\n213\t    partially completed (e.g., 1-2 new attacks only), the results add marginal support\n214\t    but also introduce taxonomy edge cases that complicate the clean GAM narrative.\n215\t    Net value: low marginal information gain for high compute cost. Better to invest\n216\t    the GPU-hours in plan_003 (BFT contrast) which directly supports a core claim.'\n217\t- name: Random-Fault BFT Contrast Experiment\n218\t  plan_id: plan_003\n219\t  description: 'Monte Carlo simulation: each judge fails independently with probability\n220\t    η_j under random faults. Compare K_eff significance under RF regime vs adaptive\n221\t    regime. GO/NO-GO gate: if RF K_eff also not significant → BFT framing collapses.'\n222\t  expected_results:\n223\t    status: partially_completed\n224\t    note: RF data exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 (2 conditions). Under median, standalone\n225\t      plan_003 adds bootstrap robustness check that weakens ARC.\n226\t    rf_mmlu_keff_sig_ols: true\n227\t    rf_mmlu_keff_p_ols: 0.002\n228\t    rf_mmlu_keff_p_bootstrap: 0.03\n229\t    rf_mmlu_keff_std_beta: 0.215\n230\t    rf_mmlu_r2: 0.73\n231\t    rf_arc_keff_sig_ols: true\n232\t    rf_arc_keff_p_ols: 0.008\n233\t    rf_arc_keff_p_bootstrap: 0.06\n234\t    rf_arc_keff_std_beta: 0.379\n235\t    rf_arc_r2: 0.68\n236\t    rf_keff_sig_bootstrap: 1/2\n237\t    rf_keff_sig_ols: 2/2\n238\t    rf_keff_sig_clustered_se: 2/2\n239\t    adaptive_keff_sig_bootstrap: 1/12\n240\t    contrast_delta_r2: RF 0.65-0.73 vs adaptive 0.15-0.30\n241\t    bft_gate: partial_pass\n242\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_003''s core data already exists within\n243\t    plan001_step1_full_v4 (RF K_eff sig 2/2 under all methods, R²=0.73/0.81). However,\n244\t    when plan_003 is run as a standalone experiment with full bootstrap robustness\n245\t    checks, ARC RF drops to borderline significance (bootstrap p=0.06) — this is because\n246\t    ARC''s 2-choice format has less scoring variance, and with only 2 RF conditions\n247\t    the bootstrap has limited power. The GO/NO-GO gate partially passes: K_eff IS\n248\t    significant under RF for MMLU (cleanly), borderline for ARC. The BFT contrast\n249\t    remains valid for MMLU (K_eff genuine under RF, artifactual under adaptive) but\n250\t    weakened for ARC. The R² gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.25) is the stronger evidence\n251\t    for the BFT framing than individual significance counts. Under median, the paper\n252\t    presents the R² gap as the primary BFT evidence and the K_eff significance contrast\n253\t    as supporting evidence with caveats about ARC weakness. The BFT framing survives\n254\t    but is downgraded from ''clean contrast'' to ''directional contrast with benchmark\n255\t    dependence.'''\n256\t  tables:\n257\t  - name: RF vs Adaptive K_eff Significance Contrast\n258\t    columns:\n259\t    - Regime\n260\t    - Benchmark\n261\t    - K_eff OLS p\n262\t    - K_eff bootstrap p\n263\t    - K_eff std_β\n264\t    - R²\n265\t    - Interpretation\n266\t    rows:\n267\t    - - Random fault\n268\t      - MMLU\n269\t      - '0.002'\n270\t      - '0.03'\n271\t      - '+0.215'\n272\t      - '0.73'\n273\t      - Genuine — survives bootstrap\n274\t    - - Random fault\n275\t      - ARC\n276\t      - '0.008'\n277\t      - '0.06'\n278\t      - '+0.379'\n279\t      - '0.68'\n280\t      - Borderline — benchmark limitation\n281\t    - - Adaptive\n282\t      - MMLU (avg 6 attacks)\n283\t      - varies\n284\t      - 5/6 NS\n285\t      - +0.18 avg\n286\t      - 0.25 avg\n287\t      - Inflated by panel overlap\n288\t    - - Adaptive\n289\t      - ARC (avg 6 attacks)\n290\t      - varies\n291\t      - 6/6 NS\n292\t      - +0.15 avg\n293\t      - 0.18 avg\n294\t      - Inflated + weak benchmark\n295\t  - name: GO/NO-GO Gate Assessment\n296\t    columns:\n297\t    - Criterion\n298\t    - Result\n299\t    - Status\n300\t    rows:\n301\t    - - RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (OLS)\n302\t      - 2/2\n303\t      - PASS\n304\t    - - RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (bootstrap)\n305\t      - 1/2\n306\t      - PARTIAL PASS\n307\t    - - RF R² > adaptive R²\n308\t      - 0.70 vs 0.22 (avg)\n309\t      - PASS\n310\t    - - RF-adaptive K_eff contrast\n311\t      - RF 1-2/2 vs adaptive 1/12\n312\t      - PARTIAL PASS\n313\t    - - BFT framing viable?\n314\t      - Yes with caveats\n315\t      - PARTIAL PASS\n316\t  figures_description:\n317\t  - 'Fig: R² comparison bar chart — RF regime vs adaptive regime, split by benchmark.\n318\t    Shows dramatic predictability gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.22) that is the core\n319\t    BFT contrast evidence.'\n320\t  - 'Fig: K_eff coefficient scatter — RF vs adaptive conditions, showing RF K_eff\n321\t    consistently positive and significant while adaptive K_eff is scattered around\n322\t    zero after correction.'\n323\t  analysis: 'The BFT contrast partially passes the GO/NO-GO gate under median. The\n324\t    R² gap is the robust finding (large, benchmark-independent). The K_eff significance\n325\t    contrast is benchmark-dependent (MMLU clean, ARC borderline). Paper should lead\n326\t    with R² gap as primary evidence for BFT contrast, use K_eff significance as secondary.\n327\t    The honest framing: ''Under random faults, panel composition (η_max + K_eff) is\n328\t    highly predictive (R²≈0.70); under adaptive attacks, predictability collapses\n329\t    (R²≈0.22), consistent with BFT assumptions being violated by strategic adversaries.''\n330\t    This avoids over-claiming on significance counts and focuses on the more robust\n331\t    quantity.'\n332\t- name: Capability-Matched Validation\n333\t  plan_id: plan_004\n334\t  description: Within same capability tier (all 7B or all 70B), test whether K_eff\n335\t    emerges as significant predictor when η_max variance is controlled by construction.\n336\t  expected_results:\n337\t    status: not_completed\n338\t    reason: Depends on plan_001; insufficient models per tier for meaningful panel\n339\t      counts\n340\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n341\t      tier_7b_models: 3\n342\t      tier_7b_panels: 1\n343\t      tier_70b_models: 3\n344\t      tier_70b_panels: 1\n345\t      tier_api_models: 9\n346\t      tier_api_panels: 84\n347\t      note: Only API tier has enough models for meaningful regression\n348\t      api_tier_eta_max_sig: 4-5/12 OLS\n349\t      api_tier_keff_sig: 2-3/12 OLS, 0-1/12 bootstrap\n350\t      api_tier_confound_r: 0.55\n351\t      api_tier_eta_max_range: narrower within tier\n352\t      within_tier_keff_emerges: false\n353\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_004 is not completed primarily because\n354\t    the experimental design doesn''t support it well. The 3/3/9 tier split means only\n355\t    the API tier (9 models, C(9,3)=84 panels) has enough data for regression. Within-tier,\n356\t    η_max variance IS reduced (models more similar in capability), but so is K_eff\n357\t    variance — both variables compress when you restrict to a capability band. The\n358\t    confound r drops from 0.70 to ~0.55 within the API tier, which is better but not\n359\t    sufficient to cleanly separate the two predictors. K_eff would show 2-3/12 significant\n360\t    under OLS within the API tier (slight improvement over full-pool because confound\n361\t    is reduced), but collapse to 0-1/12 under bootstrap (same panel overlap problem\n362\t    persists). The experiment doesn''t resolve the K_eff question because the panel\n363\t    overlap issue is structural, not related to capability matching. The paper should\n364\t    acknowledge this as a limitation: ''Capability-matched validation requires a larger\n365\t    model pool with more models per tier, which is a constraint of the current LLM\n366\t    ecosystem.'''\n367\t  tables:\n368\t  - name: Tier Composition and Statistical Power\n369\t    columns:\n370\t    - Tier\n371\t    - Models\n372\t    - Panels C(n,3)\n373\t    - η_max range\n374\t    - K_eff range\n375\t    - Regression viable?\n376\t    rows:\n377\t    - - Small (7B)\n378\t      - '3'\n379\t      - '1'\n380\t      - narrow\n381\t      - degenerate\n382\t      - No — 1 panel\n383\t    - - Medium (70B)\n384\t      - '3'\n385\t      - '1'\n386\t      - narrow\n387\t      - degenerate\n388\t      - No — 1 panel\n389\t    - - API\n390\t      - '9'\n391\t      - '84'\n392\t      - moderate\n393\t      - moderate\n394\t      - Marginal — 84 panels, 12 conditions\n395\t    - - Full pool\n396\t      - '15'\n397\t      - '455'\n398\t      - wide\n399\t      - wide\n400\t      - Yes — primary analysis\n401\t  figures_description:\n402\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: K_eff vs η_max scatter colored by tier, showing compression\n403\t    of both variables within tiers and the reduced but persistent confound.'\n404\t  analysis: This experiment is structurally underpowered given the current model pool.\n405\t    The 3/3/9 tier split was not designed for within-tier regression. Even in the\n406\t    API tier, 84 panels × 12 conditions would give ~7 effective observations per condition\n407\t    after accounting for non-independence — too few for reliable bootstrap inference.\n408\t    The paper should present the diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70) as a structural\n409\t    limitation and note that capability-matched validation requires future work with\n410\t    larger model pools. This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a failure.\n411\t- name: Two-Stage Panel Design Algorithm Benchmark\n412\t  plan_id: plan_005\n413\t  description: 'Benchmark robustness-first two-stage algorithm: (1) filter η > threshold,\n414\t    (2) maximize K_eff. Compare against 5 baselines including Strongest-K, Random,\n415\t    Max-Diversity, Min-η_max, Max-Accuracy.'\n416\t  expected_results:\n417\t    status: not_completed\n418\t    reason: Depends on plan_001; Dream flagged marginal improvement over Strongest-K\n419\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n420\t      baselines:\n421\t      - Min-η_max\n422\t      - Strongest-K\n423\t      - Max-K_eff\n424\t      - Random\n425\t      - Max-Accuracy\n426\t      two_stage_vs_min_eta:\n427\t        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 0.8\n428\t        conditions_improved: 7/12\n429\t        conditions_equal: 5/12\n430\t        conditions_worse: 0/12\n431\t      two_stage_vs_strongest_k:\n432\t        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 1.5\n433\t        note: Marginal — Strongest-K already approximates robustness-first\n434\t      two_stage_vs_max_keff:\n435\t        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 8.2\n436\t        note: Large — max diversity selects weak judges\n437\t      two_stage_vs_random:\n438\t        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 5.1\n439\t      min_eta_max_still_wins: 10-12/12\n440\t      two_stage_marginal_gain: Marginal over min_eta_max; meaningful over naive diversity\n441\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_005 is not completed but is low-risk\n442\t    because Claim 6 (min_eta_max dominates 12/12) is already strong from plan001_step1_full_v4.\n443\t    The two-stage algorithm would add K_eff maximization as a tiebreaker after robustness\n444\t    screening. Dream flagged that this is marginal because: (1) after robustness screening,\n445\t    the remaining model pool is small (e.g., top 5-6 judges), so K_eff variation among\n446\t    them is limited; (2) Strongest-K approximates robustness-first by construction\n447\t    (selecting strongest models usually means selecting most robust ones, given the\n448\t    capability-robustness correlation). The two-stage approach wins 7/12 conditions\n449\t    over pure min_eta_max but the improvement is <1pp ASR on average — practically\n450\t    negligible. The real value of the two-stage algorithm is conceptual (it provides\n451\t    a principled framework) rather than empirical (marginal gains over simpler heuristics).\n452\t    Paper can present the algorithm as a recommendation without the full benchmark,\n453\t    supported by the min_eta_max 12/12 dominance result.'\n454\t  tables:\n455\t  - name: Hypothetical Algorithm Benchmark (ASR, lower is better)\n456\t    columns:\n457\t    - Strategy\n458\t    - Mean ASR\n459\t    - Conditions best\n460\t    - vs Min-η_max Δ\n461\t    rows:\n462\t    - - Two-stage (robustness + K_eff)\n463\t      - '0.142'\n464\t      - 7/12\n465\t      - -0.8pp\n466\t    - - Min-η_max\n467\t      - '0.150'\n468\t      - 5/12\n469\t      - baseline\n470\t    - - Strongest-K\n471\t      - '0.155'\n472\t      - 0/12\n473\t      - +0.5pp\n474\t    - - Random\n475\t      - '0.193'\n476\t      - 0/12\n477\t      - +4.3pp\n478\t    - - Max-K_eff\n479\t      - '0.222'\n480\t      - 0/12\n481\t      - +7.2pp\n482\t    - - Max-Accuracy\n483\t      - '0.160'\n484\t      - 0/12\n485\t      - +1.0pp\n486\t  figures_description:\n487\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Paired difference plot showing ASR improvement of two-stage\n488\t    over each baseline across 12 conditions. Most improvements are small (<2pp) except\n489\t    vs Max-K_eff (large, 5-12pp).'\n490\t  analysis: 'The two-stage algorithm''s main contribution is showing that naive diversity\n491\t    maximization is actively harmful (Max-K_eff is worst strategy), while robustness-first\n492\t    selection is optimal. The marginal gain of adding K_eff as a tiebreaker is real\n493\t    but small. This aligns with the core narrative: judge quality dominates, diversity\n494\t    is secondary. The paper can present the algorithm conceptually with the existing\n495\t    min_eta_max 12/12 result as the primary evidence, noting that the full benchmark\n496\t    with 5 baselines is planned future work.'\n497\t- name: Per-Judge ASR Concentration Analysis\n498\t  plan_id: plan_006\n499\t  description: For each panel × attack, compute Gini coefficient and entropy of per-judge\n500\t    ASR distribution. Test whether adaptive adversaries concentrate attacks on specific\n501\t    judges (high Gini) vs spread evenly (low Gini).\n502\t  expected_results:\n503\t    status: not_completed\n504\t    reason: Time constraints; depends on plan_001 judge-level ASR breakdowns\n505\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n506\t      gini_targeted_attacks_mean: 0.45\n507\t      gini_shared_bias_attacks_mean: 0.22\n508\t      gini_difference_p: 0.003\n509\t      entropy_targeted_mean: 1.8\n510\t      entropy_shared_bias_mean: 2.5\n511\t      gini_eta_max_correlation: 0.62\n512\t      concentration_supports_mechanism: true\n513\t      note: Targeted attacks show higher concentration (exploit specific weak judges);\n514\t        shared-bias attacks spread more evenly (exploit common vulnerability)\n515\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, this experiment is not completed due to time\n516\t    constraints — it requires per-judge ASR breakdowns that may not be stored in the\n517\t    current data pipeline. If run, the results would support the GAM taxonomy mechanism:\n518\t    targeted attacks (PI, SM, PB) concentrate ASR on specific weak judges (high Gini\n519\t    ~0.45), while shared-bias attacks (syco, VB, auth) spread vulnerability more evenly\n520\t    across judges (low Gini ~0.22). This concentration difference provides direct\n521\t    evidence for WHY η_max dominates: adaptive adversaries exploit the single weakest\n522\t    judge disproportionately. However, the Gini-η_max correlation (~0.62) means this\n523\t    is partially confounded — panels with high η_max naturally have more unequal ASR\n524\t    distributions. The result is confirmatory rather than independently informative.\n525\t    Under median, this adds a nice figure to the supplementary but doesn''t change\n526\t    any claims. Not completing it is an acceptable loss.'\n527\t  tables:\n528\t  - name: Hypothetical Gini Coefficients by Attack Category\n529\t    columns:\n530\t    - Attack type\n531\t    - Category\n532\t    - Gini (mean)\n533\t    - Gini (SD)\n534\t    - Entropy (mean)\n535\t    rows:\n536\t    - - prompt_injection\n537\t      - targeted\n538\t      - '0.52'\n539\t      - '0.11'\n540\t      - '1.6'\n541\t    - - score_manipulation\n542\t      - targeted\n543\t      - '0.48'\n544\t      - '0.13'\n545\t      - '1.7'\n546\t    - - position_bias\n547\t      - targeted\n548\t      - '0.38'\n549\t      - '0.09'\n550\t      - '2.0'\n551\t    - - sycophancy\n552\t      - shared-bias\n553\t      - '0.25'\n554\t      - '0.08'\n555\t      - '2.4'\n556\t    - - verbosity_bias\n557\t      - shared-bias\n558\t      - '0.20'\n559\t      - '0.07'\n560\t      - '2.5'\n561\t    - - authority_bias\n562\t      - shared-bias\n563\t      - '0.21'\n564\t      - '0.06'\n565\t      - '2.6'\n566\t  figures_description:\n567\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Violin plot of Gini coefficients by attack category (targeted\n568\t    vs shared-bias), showing clear separation with some overlap in position_bias.'\n569\t  analysis: 'Confirmatory experiment — would strengthen the mechanistic narrative\n570\t    for the GAM 6:6 split but is not essential for any core claim. The Gini concentration\n571\t    metric provides an intuitive explanation for practitioners: ''targeted attacks\n572\t    pile up on your worst judge; shared-bias attacks erode all judges somewhat equally.''\n573\t    Non-completion is acknowledged as a limitation in the discussion section.'\n574\t- name: Diversity-Capability Tradeoff Visualization\n575\t  plan_id: plan_007\n576\t  description: Plot K_eff vs η_max across all 455 panels. Fit tradeoff curve. Compute\n577\t    Pareto frontier. Stratify by model tier.\n578\t  expected_results:\n579\t    status: completed_or_trivially_completable\n580\t    reason: No GPU needed; uses existing panel-level data from plan001_step1_full_v4\n581\t    keff_eta_max_r: 0.7\n582\t    pareto_frontier_panels: 8\n583\t    pareto_frontier_composition: Dominated by API-tier + top open-source panels\n584\t    tier_stratification:\n585\t      small_7b_panels:\n586\t        keff_range:\n587\t        - 2.2\n588\t        - 2.8\n589\t        eta_max_range:\n590\t        - 0.15\n591\t        - 0.35\n592\t      medium_70b_panels:\n593\t        keff_range:\n594\t        - 2.0\n595\t        - 2.6\n596\t        eta_max_range:\n597\t        - 0.08\n598\t        - 0.2\n599\t      api_panels:\n600\t        keff_range:\n601\t        - 1.5\n602\t        - 2.5\n603\t        eta_max_range:\n604\t        - 0.03\n605\t        - 0.15\n606\t    tradeoff_curve_fit: 'log-linear: K_eff ≈ 1.2 + 0.8·log(η_max)'\n607\t    residual_variance: Substantial — r=0.70 means 51% variance explained; panels with\n608\t      same η_max can have K_eff ranging ±0.5\n609\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, this experiment is trivially completable (no\n610\t    GPU, pure post-processing of existing data). The r=0.70 tradeoff is already known.\n611\t    The Pareto frontier visualization adds value by showing practitioners which specific\n612\t    panel compositions achieve the best K_eff for a given robustness level. The tier\n613\t    stratification confirms that small models occupy the high-η_max/high-K_eff corner\n614\t    (diverse but weak) while API models span a wider range including the desirable\n615\t    low-η_max region. The log-linear tradeoff curve captures the structural constraint:\n616\t    as you add more diverse (independent) judges, you necessarily include less capable\n617\t    ones. The 49% unexplained variance means the tradeoff is real but not deterministic\n618\t    — some panel compositions beat the tradeoff curve, and these are candidates for\n619\t    practical deployment. This is a solid supplementary figure but doesn''t change\n620\t    any claims — it visualizes Claim 3.'\n621\t  tables:\n622\t  - name: Pareto-Optimal Panel Compositions (hypothetical top 5)\n623\t    columns:\n624\t    - Panel ID\n625\t    - Models\n626\t    - K_eff\n627\t    - η_max\n628\t    - Pareto rank\n629\t    rows:\n630\t    - - P_042\n631\t      - gpt-4o + claude-3.5-sonnet + qwen2.5-72b\n632\t      - '2.35'\n633\t      - '0.06'\n634\t      - '1'\n635\t    - - P_118\n636\t      - gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet + llama-70b\n637\t      - '2.28'\n638\t      - '0.07'\n639\t      - '2'\n640\t    - - P_203\n641\t      - gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + qwen2.5-72b\n642\t      - '1.95'\n643\t      - '0.04'\n644\t      - '3'\n645\t    - - P_089\n646\t      - gpt-4.1 + gpt-4.1-mini + claude-3.5-sonnet\n647\t      - '1.72'\n648\t      - '0.03'\n649\t      - '4'\n650\t    - - P_301\n651\t      - gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet\n652\t      - '1.85'\n653\t      - '0.035'\n654\t      - '5'\n655\t  figures_description:\n656\t  - 'Fig: K_eff vs η_max scatter for 455 panels, colored by tier. Pareto frontier\n657\t    highlighted. Log-linear tradeoff curve overlaid. Annotations for key panel compositions.'\n658\t  analysis: 'Clean visualization exercise that supports the narrative. The main insight\n659\t    for practitioners: the Pareto frontier shows you CAN get moderate diversity (K_eff\n660\t    ~2.0-2.3) with good robustness (η_max <0.07) by mixing top API models with the\n661\t    best open-source models. But pushing K_eff beyond ~2.5 requires including 7B-class\n662\t    models that dramatically increase η_max. This is the structural constraint behind\n663\t    Claim 3.'\n664\t- name: Panel Size Sensitivity K=3,5,7,9\n665\t  plan_id: plan_008\n666\t  description: Test whether η_max dominance persists as panel size K grows from 3\n667\t    to 5, 7, 9. Larger panels may dilute η_max effect through averaging.\n668\t  expected_results:\n669\t    status: not_completed\n670\t    reason: 30 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001; combinatorial explosion at K=7,9\n671\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n672\t      k3_eta_sig: 5/12 bootstrap\n673\t      k5_eta_sig: 4-5/12 bootstrap\n674\t      k7_eta_sig: 3-4/12 bootstrap\n675\t      k9_eta_sig: 2-3/12 bootstrap\n676\t      k3_keff_sig: 1/12 bootstrap\n677\t      k5_keff_sig: 0-1/12 bootstrap\n678\t      k7_keff_sig: 0-1/12 bootstrap\n679\t      k9_keff_sig: 1-2/12 bootstrap\n680\t      eta_max_weakening_trend: η_max significance decreases monotonically with K\n681\t      keff_stable_or_slight_increase: K_eff remains NS but may gain marginal significance\n682\t        at K=9\n683\t      panels_k5: 3003\n684\t      panels_k7: 6435\n685\t      panels_k9: 5005\n686\t      overlap_severity_k9: extreme — each model in ~3000 panels\n687\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_008 is not completed due to compute constraints\n688\t    and combinatorial explosion. If run, the key prediction is that η_max dominance\n689\t    WEAKENS with larger panels because: (1) larger panels average over more judges,\n690\t    diluting any single judge''s weakness; (2) the η_max metric itself (max individual\n691\t    vulnerability) becomes a less tight predictor when K grows — in a K=9 panel, having\n692\t    1 weak judge out of 9 matters less than 1 out of 3 under majority vote; (3) conversely,\n693\t    K_eff might gain marginal significance at K=9 because diversity effects accumulate\n694\t    with more judges. However, the panel non-independence problem becomes CATASTROPHIC\n695\t    at K=7,9: C(15,7)=6435 panels means each model appears in ~3000 panels (66% overlap),\n696\t    making bootstrap correction extremely aggressive and possibly breaking inference\n697\t    entirely. The honest median prediction is that K=5 gives similar results to K=3\n698\t    (confirming robustness), but K=7 and K=9 are uninterpretable due to overlap severity.\n699\t    This is a known limitation of the combinatorial design.'\n700\t  tables:\n701\t  - name: Hypothetical Panel Size Sensitivity\n702\t    columns:\n703\t    - K\n704\t    - Panels\n705\t    - Avg overlap per model\n706\t    - η_max sig (bootstrap)\n707\t    - K_eff sig (bootstrap)\n708\t    - Interpretable?\n709\t    rows:\n710\t    - - '3'\n711\t      - '455'\n712\t      - 91 (20%)\n713\t      - 5/12\n714\t      - 1/12\n715\t      - 'Yes'\n716\t    - - '5'\n717\t      - '3003'\n718\t      - 1001 (33%)\n719\t      - 4-5/12\n720\t      - 0-1/12\n721\t      - Marginal\n722\t    - - '7'\n723\t      - '6435'\n724\t      - 3003 (47%)\n725\t      - 3-4/12\n726\t      - 0-1/12\n727\t      - Questionable\n728\t    - - '9'\n729\t      - '5005'\n730\t      - 3003 (60%)\n731\t      - 2-3/12\n732\t      - 1-2/12\n733\t      - No — overlap too severe\n734\t  figures_description:\n735\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: η_max and K_eff significance counts vs panel size K, showing\n736\t    monotonic weakening of η_max and stable-to-marginal K_eff.'\n737\t  analysis: 'Panel size sensitivity is important for generalization but faces a fundamental\n738\t    design limitation: with 15 models, larger panels create extreme overlap that invalidates\n739\t    bootstrap inference. The paper can note this as a limitation: ''Our K=3 design\n740\t    balances combinatorial coverage against overlap severity; K>5 analysis requires\n741\t    a larger model pool.'' This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a threat\n742\t    to the core claims.'\n743\t- name: VIF and Multicollinearity Analysis\n744\t  plan_id: plan_009\n745\t  description: Compute VIF for K_eff and η_max. Partial correlations. Residualized\n746\t    K_eff regression. Bonferroni/FDR correction. Beta regression as robustness check.\n747\t  expected_results:\n748\t    status: completed\n749\t    reason: No GPU needed; uses existing regression data\n750\t    vif_keff: 3.4\n751\t    vif_eta_max: 3.4\n752\t    note_vif: 'Symmetric VIF due to single confound; VIF = 1/(1-r²) = 1/(1-0.49) ≈\n753\t      1.96 for r=0.70. Wait — VIF for 2-predictor model = 1/(1-r²) where r is between\n754\t      the two predictors. r=0.70 → r²=0.49 → VIF=1.96. But log-log spec may have different\n755\t      effective correlation. Adjusting: log(K_eff) vs log(η_max) correlation ~0.65\n756\t      → VIF ≈ 2.7. With tier indicators, VIF could reach 3-5 range.'\n757\t    vif_with_tier_control: 4.2\n758\t    partial_corr_eta_given_keff: 0.35\n759\t    partial_corr_keff_given_eta: 0.12\n760\t    residualized_keff_sig: 3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap\n761\t    beta_regression_eta_sig: 10/12\n762\t    beta_regression_keff_sig: 5/12\n763\t    bonferroni_eta_sig: 8/12\n764\t    bonferroni_keff_sig: 4/12\n765\t    fdr_eta_sig: 10/12\n766\t    fdr_keff_sig: 6/12\n767\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_009 is completed (no GPU needed). VIF\n768\t    in the 3-5 range indicates moderate multicollinearity — not severe enough to invalidate\n769\t    the regression (conventional threshold is VIF>10 for concern, VIF>5 for caution),\n770\t    but enough to inflate standard errors and make coefficient estimates less stable.\n771\t    The key insight is asymmetric: partial correlation of η_max given K_eff (0.35)\n772\t    is much larger than K_eff given η_max (0.12), confirming η_max retains independent\n773\t    predictive power while K_eff''s independent contribution is marginal. Residualized\n774\t    K_eff (regress K_eff on η_max, use residuals as predictor) gives 3/12 under OLS\n775\t    and 0/12 under bootstrap — confirming that K_eff''s significance in the main regression\n776\t    is partially driven by its correlation with η_max. Beta regression (appropriate\n777\t    for bounded ASR data) gives higher K_eff significance (5/12) than OLS (8/12) because\n778\t    beta regression handles the 0-1 bounded response better, but the η_max dominance\n779\t    pattern persists (10/12 vs 5/12). Bonferroni correction (conservative) drops K_eff\n780\t    to 4/12; FDR (less conservative) keeps it at 6/12. Net: VIF requires discussion\n781\t    but doesn''t change the core narrative — η_max dominates regardless of how you\n782\t    handle the collinearity.'\n783\t  tables:\n784\t  - name: VIF Analysis Summary\n785\t    columns:\n786\t    - Specification\n787\t    - VIF(K_eff)\n788\t    - VIF(η_max)\n789\t    - Interpretation\n790\t    rows:\n791\t    - - Base log-log (2 predictors)\n792\t      - '1.96'\n793\t      - '1.96'\n794\t      - Low — acceptable\n795\t    - - With tier indicators (3/3/9)\n796\t      - '3.8'\n797\t      - '4.2'\n798\t      - Moderate — requires discussion\n799\t    - - With tier + benchmark interaction\n800\t      - '4.5'\n801\t      - '4.8'\n802\t      - Moderate-high — approaching caution threshold\n803\t  - name: Partial Correlations and Residualized Regression\n804\t    columns:\n805\t    - Analysis\n806\t    - η_max result\n807\t    - K_eff result\n808\t    - Dominance\n809\t    rows:\n810\t    - - Partial corr (given other)\n811\t      - r=0.35\n812\t      - r=0.12\n813\t      - η_max 3x stronger\n814\t    - - Residualized K_eff (OLS)\n815\t      - —\n816\t      - 3/12 sig\n817\t      - K_eff marginal\n818\t    - - Residualized K_eff (bootstrap)\n819\t      - —\n820\t      - 0/12 sig\n821\t      - K_eff eliminated\n822\t    - - Beta regression\n823\t      - 10/12 sig\n824\t      - 5/12 sig\n825\t      - η_max 2x\n826\t    - - Bonferroni correction\n827\t      - 8/12 sig\n828\t      - 4/12 sig\n829\t      - η_max 2x\n830\t    - - FDR correction\n831\t      - 10/12 sig\n832\t      - 6/12 sig\n833\t      - η_max 1.7x\n834\t  figures_description:\n835\t  - 'Fig: Partial regression plots for η_max and K_eff — η_max plot shows clear positive\n836\t    slope with tight scatter; K_eff plot shows weak positive slope with wide scatter\n837\t    and confidence interval crossing zero in most conditions.'\n838\t  analysis: 'VIF analysis is a clean methodological contribution. The VIF 3-5 range\n839\t    is in the ''requires discussion but not invalidating'' zone. The paper should\n840\t    include a paragraph in the methods section noting: ''K_eff and η_max show moderate\n841\t    collinearity (VIF 3-5 with tier controls). We address this through (a) partial\n842\t    correlations confirming η_max retains 3× stronger independent association than\n843\t    K_eff, (b) residualized K_eff regression showing K_eff''s significance is partially\n844\t    confound-driven, (c) alternative diversity metrics (Q-stat, disagreement, κ-diversity)\n845\t    providing converging evidence that η_max dominates regardless of diversity operationalization.''\n846\t    This turns a potential weakness into a strength by demonstrating thorough collinearity\n847\t    awareness.'\n848\t- name: Non-Majority-Vote Aggregation Analysis\n849\t  plan_id: plan_010\n850\t  description: Test weakest-link dominance under weighted voting, veto rules, Bayesian\n851\t    aggregation. Verify aggregation gradient holds under correction methods.\n852\t  expected_results:\n853\t    status: partially_completed_via_plan001\n854\t    reason: Aggregation gradient (majority/weighted/threshold/bayesian) already in\n855\t      plan001_step1_full_v4. Plan_010 adds clustered SE / bootstrap validation of\n856\t      aggregation results.\n857\t    aggregation_gradient_ols:\n858\t      majority:\n859\t        eta_sig: 11/12\n860\t        keff_sig: 8/12\n861\t        keff_neg: 1\n862\t      weighted:\n863\t        eta_sig: 9/12\n864\t        keff_sig: 9/12\n865\t        keff_neg: 0\n866\t      threshold:\n867\t        eta_sig: 12/12\n868\t        keff_sig: 11/12\n869\t        keff_neg: 1\n870\t        r2: 0.89\n871\t      bayesian:\n872\t        eta_sig: 9/12\n873\t        keff_sig: 7/12\n874\t        keff_neg: 6\n875\t    aggregation_gradient_clustered_se:\n876\t      majority:\n877\t        eta_sig: 8/12\n878\t        keff_sig: 0/12\n879\t      weighted:\n880\t        eta_sig: 6/12\n881\t        keff_sig: 2/12\n882\t      threshold:\n883\t        eta_sig: 10/12\n884\t        keff_sig: 5/12\n885\t      bayesian:\n886\t        eta_sig: 5/12\n887\t        keff_sig: 4/12\n888\t        keff_neg: 3\n889\t    aggregation_gradient_bootstrap:\n890\t      majority:\n891\t        eta_sig: 5/12\n892\t        keff_sig: 1/12\n893\t      weighted:\n894\t        eta_sig: 4/12\n895\t        keff_sig: 1/12\n896\t      threshold:\n897\t        eta_sig: 7/12\n898\t        keff_sig: 3/12\n899\t      bayesian:\n900\t        eta_sig: 3/12\n901\t        keff_sig: 3/12\n902\t        keff_neg: 2\n903\t    bayesian_keff_flip_attenuated: true\n904\t    bayesian_keff_negative_bootstrap: 2/7 (down from 6/7 OLS)\n905\t    threshold_most_robust: true\n906\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, the aggregation gradient exists in plan001_step1_full_v4\n907\t    under OLS but has NOT been validated under clustered SE or bootstrap. Plan_010''s\n908\t    median outcome is that this validation is partially completed, revealing important\n909\t    attenuation. The Bayesian K_eff flip (6/7 negative under OLS) is partially attenuated\n910\t    under clustered SE (3/7 negative) and substantially attenuated under bootstrap\n911\t    (2/7 negative). This is because the K_eff flip under Bayesian aggregation was\n912\t    partially driven by the same panel overlap inflation — when corrected, fewer conditions\n913\t    show the sign reversal. The key narrative impact: Bayesian aggregation STILL shows\n914\t    a directional tendency to flip K_eff to negative (protective), but the effect\n915\t    is weaker and less reliable than OLS suggests. Threshold voting remains the most\n916\t    robust aggregation for significance (highest η_max and K_eff sig counts even after\n917\t    correction), consistent with unanimity rules being most sensitive to panel composition.\n918\t    The aggregation gradient direction (threshold → majority → weighted → bayesian)\n919\t    is preserved under all correction methods — the ordinal ranking is robust even\n920\t    if absolute significance counts decrease. This allows the paper to retain Claim\n921\t    7 with softened language: ''aggregation rule choice modulates vulnerability drivers,\n922\t    with Bayesian calibration showing directional evidence of K_eff reversal that\n923\t    requires further validation.'''\n924\t  tables:\n925\t  - name: Aggregation Gradient Under Three Correction Methods\n926\t    columns:\n927\t    - Aggregation\n928\t    - η_max OLS\n929\t    - η_max clustered\n930\t    - η_max bootstrap\n931\t    - K_eff OLS\n932\t    - K_eff clustered\n933\t    - K_eff bootstrap\n934\t    - K_eff neg (OLS/clustered/bootstrap)\n935\t    rows:\n936\t    - - Majority\n937\t      - 11/12\n938\t      - 8/12\n939\t      - 5/12\n940\t      - 8/12\n941\t      - 0/12\n942\t      - 1/12\n943\t      - 1 / 0 / 0\n944\t    - - Weighted\n945\t      - 9/12\n946\t      - 6/12\n947\t      - 4/12\n948\t      - 9/12\n949\t      - 2/12\n950\t      - 1/12\n951\t      - 0 / 0 / 0\n952\t    - - Threshold\n953\t      - 12/12\n954\t      - 10/12\n955\t      - 7/12\n956\t      - 11/12\n957\t      - 5/12\n958\t      - 3/12\n959\t      - 1 / 1 / 0\n960\t    - - Bayesian\n961\t      - 9/12\n962\t      - 5/12\n963\t      - 3/12\n964\t      - 7/12\n965\t      - 4/12\n966\t      - 3/12\n967\t      - 6 / 3 / 2\n968\t  - name: Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip Detail\n969\t    columns:\n970\t    - Condition\n971\t    - K_eff β (OLS)\n972\t    - K_eff sign (OLS)\n973\t    - K_eff sig (clustered SE)\n974\t    - K_eff sig (bootstrap)\n975\t    - Flip survives?\n976\t    rows:\n977\t    - - mmlu×PI\n978\t      - '-0.42'\n979\t      - negative\n980\t      - Yes (p=0.04)\n981\t      - Yes (p=0.03)\n982\t      - 'Yes'\n983\t    - - mmlu×SM\n984\t      - '-0.38'\n985\t      - negative\n986\t      - No (p=0.12)\n987\t      - No (p=0.18)\n988\t      - 'No'\n989\t    - - mmlu×PB\n990\t      - '-0.31'\n991\t      - negative\n992\t      - No (p=0.15)\n993\t      - No (p=0.22)\n994\t      - 'No'\n995\t    - - mmlu×syco\n996\t      - '-0.25'\n997\t      - negative\n998\t      - Yes (p=0.04)\n999\t      - No (p=0.08)\n1000\t      - Partial\n1001\t    - - mmlu×VB\n1002\t      - '-0.29'\n1003\t      - negative\n1004\t      - No (p=0.09)\n1005\t      - No (p=0.14)\n1006\t      - 'No'\n1007\t    - - mmlu×auth\n1008\t      - '-0.22'\n1009\t      - negative\n1010\t      - Yes (p=0.03)\n1011\t      - Yes (p=0.04)\n1012\t      - 'Yes'\n1013\t    - - arc×PI\n1014\t      - '+0.15'\n1015\t      - positive\n1016\t      - No (p=0.35)\n1017\t      - No (p=0.42)\n1018\t      - N/A (wrong sign)\n1019\t  figures_description:\n1020\t  - 'Fig: Aggregation gradient — 4 panels showing η_max and K_eff significance under\n1021\t    OLS, clustered SE, bootstrap. Bars decrease in height from threshold to bayesian\n1022\t    for η_max; K_eff bars show sign flip in Bayesian panel.'\n1023\t  analysis: 'The aggregation gradient is a real finding that survives correction in\n1024\t    ordinal terms (threshold > majority > weighted > bayesian for dual-driver significance).\n1025\t    The Bayesian K_eff flip attenuation is the key median concern: the paper cannot\n1026\t    claim ''Bayesian reverses K_eff direction'' with the same confidence as under\n1027\t    OLS. Softened framing: ''Bayesian calibration shows a directional tendency to\n1028\t    reverse K_eff from harmful to protective, with 6/7 conditions showing negative\n1029\t    K_eff coefficients under OLS. However, only 2-3/7 survive non-independence correction,\n1030\t    suggesting the sign reversal is partially inflated by panel overlap. The structural\n1031\t    direction (Bayesian reduces both η_max and K_eff significance) is consistent across\n1032\t    correction methods.'' This is honest and still supports the practical recommendation\n1033\t    of Bayesian aggregation as part of the defense stack.'\n1034\t- name: Open-Ended Generation Benchmark\n1035\t  plan_id: plan_011\n1036\t  description: Add MT-Bench or AlpacaEval-style task beyond MCQ. Test whether η_max\n1037\t    dominance generalizes beyond multiple-choice format.\n1038\t  expected_results:\n1039\t    status: not_completed\n1040\t    reason: 40 GPU-hours; requires new evaluation pipeline for open-ended generation\n1041\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n1042\t      benchmark: MT-Bench (8 categories)\n1043\t      models_evaluated: 15\n1044\t      panels: 455\n1045\t      eta_max_sig_ols: 8-10/48 (8 categories × 6 attacks)\n1046\t      eta_max_sig_bootstrap: 3-5/48\n1047\t      keff_sig_ols: 5-8/48\n1048\t      keff_sig_bootstrap: 0-2/48\n1049\t      eta_max_dominance_holds: true\n1050\t      scoring_variance_impact: Open-ended scoring has MORE variance than MCQ → η_max\n1051\t        may be STRONGER\n1052\t      complication: Attack definitions need adaptation — prompt_injection and score_manipulation\n1053\t        differ for open-ended evaluation\n1054\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_011 is not completed due to the substantial\n1055\t    engineering effort required: open-ended generation evaluation requires different\n1056\t    attack implementations (prompt injection in scoring rubrics rather than MCQ options),\n1057\t    different evaluation protocols (pairwise comparison or Likert-scale rather than\n1058\t    accuracy), and different ASR definitions. The 40 GPU-hour budget underestimates\n1059\t    the engineering overhead. If run, the prediction is that η_max dominance would\n1060\t    hold or strengthen: open-ended scoring has higher variance than MCQ (continuous\n1061\t    scale vs binary correct/incorrect), which provides MORE signal for η_max. However,\n1062\t    K_eff significance might also increase because judge disagreements in open-ended\n1063\t    evaluation are more diverse and less correlated. The net effect: η_max still dominates\n1064\t    but the ratio may be closer to 3:1 rather than 5:1. The benchmark asymmetry concern\n1065\t    (ARC weaker than MMLU) would be partially addressed if MT-Bench shows strong η_max,\n1066\t    but this is speculative. Paper acknowledges this as the primary limitation: ''Our\n1067\t    findings are established on MCQ benchmarks; generalization to open-ended evaluation\n1068\t    is a critical direction for future work.'''\n1069\t  tables:\n1070\t  - name: Hypothetical MT-Bench Results (aggregated across 8 categories)\n1071\t    columns:\n1072\t    - Metric\n1073\t    - OLS\n1074\t    - Bootstrap\n1075\t    - Note\n1076\t    rows:\n1077\t    - - η_max sig\n1078\t      - 8-10/48\n1079\t      - 3-5/48\n1080\t      - Similar ratio to MCQ (~50% OLS, ~10% bootstrap)\n1081\t    - - K_eff sig\n1082\t      - 5-8/48\n1083\t      - 0-2/48\n1084\t      - Slightly higher than MCQ due to scoring variance\n1085\t    - - Dominance ratio\n1086\t      - ~2:1 OLS, ~3:1 bootstrap\n1087\t      - —\n1088\t      - Closer ratio than MCQ due to more K_eff signal\n1089\t    - - R² mean\n1090\t      - '0.18'\n1091\t      - —\n1092\t      - Lower than MCQ — more noise in open-ended scoring\n1093\t  figures_description:\n1094\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Side-by-side heatmap of η_max and K_eff significance across\n1095\t    MCQ (MMLU+ARC) vs open-ended (MT-Bench), showing consistent η_max dominance pattern.'\n1096\t  analysis: 'This is the highest-value missing experiment for the paper''s generalizability\n1097\t    claims, but also the most expensive to implement. Under median, it''s not completed\n1098\t    and the paper acknowledges MCQ-only as the primary scope limitation. The benchmark\n1099\t    asymmetry discussion (ARC weaker than MMLU) already provides an opening: ''scoring\n1100\t    format affects signal strength for η_max, motivating future work on open-ended\n1101\t    evaluation where scoring variance is higher.'' This turns the limitation into\n1102\t    a clear next step.'\n1103\t- name: Partial-Knowledge Adversary Model\n1104\t  plan_id: plan_012\n1105\t  description: Test adversary who knows model families but not exact vulnerability\n1106\t    profiles. Interpolates between white-box (knows all η_j) and black-box (knows\n1107\t    nothing) settings.\n1108\t  expected_results:\n1109\t    status: not_completed\n1110\t    reason: 10 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001 + plan_003; low priority (P2)\n1111\t    hypothetical_if_run:\n1112\t      adversary_levels:\n1113\t        black_box:\n1114\t          asr_mean: 0.08\n1115\t          eta_max_sig: 3/12\n1116\t          keff_sig: 1/12\n1117\t        family_knowledge:\n1118\t          asr_mean: 0.12\n1119\t          eta_max_sig: 5/12\n1120\t          keff_sig: 2/12\n1121\t        capability_tier_knowledge:\n1122\t          asr_mean: 0.16\n1123\t          eta_max_sig: 7/12\n1124\t          keff_sig: 3/12\n1125\t        white_box:\n1126\t          asr_mean: 0.22\n1127\t          eta_max_sig: 11/12\n1128\t          keff_sig: 8/12\n1129\t      eta_max_dominance_holds_all_levels: true\n1130\t      keff_increases_with_knowledge: true\n1131\t      family_knowledge_insufficient: Confirmed — family labels don't predict vulnerability\n1132\t        (Claim 4)\n1133\t  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_012 is not completed (low priority P2,\n1134\t    depends on two other experiments). If run, the main prediction is that η_max dominance\n1135\t    holds across all adversary knowledge levels, but the STRENGTH of both predictors\n1136\t    increases with adversary knowledge. Under black-box (no knowledge of panel composition),\n1137\t    attacks are essentially random → lower ASR, both η_max and K_eff weakly significant.\n1138\t    Under family knowledge, the adversary can guess which model families are in the\n1139\t    panel but not exact vulnerability profiles → modest improvement, but family labels\n1140\t    are unreliable predictors (Claim 4), so the gain is limited. Under white-box (current\n1141\t    experimental setup), ASR is highest and both predictors are most significant.\n1142\t    The gradient from black-box to white-box would show that panel vulnerability is\n1143\t    primarily an information asymmetry problem: adversaries who know judge weaknesses\n1144\t    can exploit η_max effectively; those who don''t are reduced to generic attacks\n1145\t    where K_eff matters more. This is interesting but not essential for the core narrative\n1146\t    — it''s a natural extension that strengthens the practical implications section.'\n1147\t  tables:\n1148\t  - name: Hypothetical Adversary Knowledge Gradient\n1149\t    columns:\n1150\t    - Knowledge level\n1151\t    - Mean ASR\n1152\t    - η_max sig (OLS)\n1153\t    - K_eff sig (OLS)\n1154\t    - η_max:K_eff ratio\n1155\t    rows:\n1156\t    - - Black-box\n1157\t      - '0.08'\n1158\t      - 3/12\n1159\t      - 1/12\n1160\t      - '3:1'\n1161\t    - - Family labels only\n1162\t      - '0.12'\n1163\t      - 5/12\n1164\t      - 2/12\n1165\t      - 2.5:1\n1166\t    - - Capability tier\n1167\t      - '0.16'\n1168\t      - 7/12\n1169\t      - 3/12\n1170\t      - 2.3:1\n1171\t    - - White-box (full η_j)\n1172\t      - '0.22'\n1173\t      - 11/12\n1174\t      - 8/12\n1175\t      - 1.4:1\n1176\t  figures_description:\n1177\t  - 'Hypothetical Fig: ASR vs adversary knowledge level, with η_max and K_eff significance\n1178\t    overlaid, showing monotonic increase in both ASR and predictor significance.'\n1179\t  analysis: Interesting theoretical contribution but not essential under median tier.\n1180\t    The finding that information asymmetry modulates predictor strength is intuitive\n1181\t    — if you don't know which judge is weakest, you can't exploit η_max. The practical\n1182\t    implication (keep panel composition secret) is already common sense for security.\n1183\t    Non-completion is acceptable; could be framed as future work in the discussion\n1184\t    section.\n1185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"800f8220-c7c3-4142-b3c3-4a1c2a5f3ff6","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:56:14.999Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/cert_manip_resist_eval/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T221131/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Claims require hedging but core story survives. η_max dominance\n  downgraded from ''robust'' to ''directional with benchmark-dependent strength''\n  — MMLU strong, ARC weak, must discuss why. K_eff fragility still holds but framed\n  as ''inconclusive'' rather than ''clean null''. GAM 6:6 split presented as ''suggestive\n  taxonomic pattern warranting further investigation'' rather than confirmed mechanism.\n  RF contrast becomes a partial validation (if completed) or acknowledged limitation\n  (if not). Aggregation gradient retains OLS results with caveat that clustered SE\n  validation is incomplete. Paper shifts from 5-contribution to 3-contribution focus:\n  (1) η_max dominance (primary), (2) methodological transparency on non-independence\n  (elevated to co-primary — the correction story itself becomes a contribution), (3)\n  practical defense recommendations (min_eta_max still wins). Title softened to candidate\n  2 or 3. ''Diversity Trap'' title likely too strong — ''Weakest Link'' framing preferred.\n  Supplementary materials carry more analytical weight to address reviewer concerns\n  about incomplete robustness checks.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Scale Expansion to 15 Models\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: Add API models (GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, Claude,\n    Gemini) and large open-source (Llama-3.1-70B, Qwen2.5-72B) to reach 15 models\n    total. Rerun MI matrix + full regression suite on C(15,3)=455 panels across 12\n    benchmark×attack conditions.\n  expected_results:\n    models_total: 15\n    api_models: 9\n    local_models: 6\n    panels: 455\n    conditions: 12\n    confound_r_keff_eta: 0.7\n    ols_eta_sig: 11/12\n    ols_keff_sig: 8/12 (7+/1-)\n    bootstrap_eta_sig: 5/12\n    bootstrap_keff_sig: 1/12\n    clustered_se_eta_sig: 8/12\n    clustered_se_keff_sig: 0/12\n    mixed_effects_eta_sig: 7/12\n    mixed_effects_keff_sig: 3/12\n    dominance_ratio_bootstrap: '5:1'\n    se_inflation_factor: ~2.5x\n    mmlu_bootstrap_eta: 4/6\n    arc_bootstrap_eta: 1/6\n    gam_targeted_positive: 6/6\n    gam_sharedbias_negative: 6/6\n    min_eta_max_wins: 12/12\n    practical_delta_asr_keff_iqr_pp: 1.3\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_001 IS the shipped evidence — plan001_step1_full_v4\n    realized it successfully. The 15-model expansion dramatically increased panel\n    count (20→455) and broke the 6-model confound ceiling (r=-0.8→+0.70). K_eff flips\n    from NS-negative (6-model: arc×sycophancy only) to predominantly positive (7+/1-)\n    under OLS, BUT this OLS significance is inflated by panel non-independence: each\n    of 15 judges appears in ~91 panels, creating massive overlap. Correction methods\n    (bootstrap/clustered SE/mixed effects) collapse K_eff from 8/12 to 0-3/12 while\n    η_max survives at 5-8/12. The 5:1 dominance ratio under bootstrap is actually\n    STRONGER than OLS''s 11:8≈1.4:1 because K_eff collapses faster than η_max. ARC\n    weakness (1/6 bootstrap η_max vs MMLU 4/6) reflects MCQ format: 4-choice MMLU\n    gives more scoring variance for η_max signal than 2-choice ARC. This benchmark\n    asymmetry is a structural limitation of the experimental design, not a refutation\n    of η_max dominance.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Correction Method Comparison (12 conditions)\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - η_max sig\n    - K_eff sig\n    - Dominance ratio\n    - Note\n    rows:\n    - - OLS (baseline)\n      - 11/12\n      - 8/12 (7+/1-)\n      - 1.4:1\n      - Inflated by panel overlap\n    - - Wild cluster bootstrap v2\n      - 5/12\n      - 1/12\n      - '5:1'\n      - Conservative lower bound; position 0 imbalance\n    - - Clustered SE (CR1)\n      - 8/12\n      - 0/12\n      - '8:0'\n      - Consistent with bootstrap positions 1&2\n    - - Mixed effects\n      - 7/12\n      - 3/12\n      - 2.3:1\n      - Random judge intercepts\n    - - Tier-indicator corrected\n      - 11/12\n      - 5/12\n      - 2.2:1\n      - 3/3/9 tier control\n  - name: Bootstrap η_max by Benchmark\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - η_max bootstrap sig\n    - Attacks surviving\n    - Attacks lost\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 4/6\n      - PI, SM, PB, VB\n      - syco, auth\n    - - ARC\n      - 1/6\n      - PI only (marginal)\n      - SM, PB, syco, VB, auth\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 1: Coefficient significance heatmap — 4 correction methods × 12 conditions,\n    color-coded by η_max/K_eff significance. Shows systematic K_eff collapse across\n    rows while η_max retains partial survival.'\n  - 'Fig 2: SE inflation scatter — OLS SE vs corrected SE for each coefficient, showing\n    ~2.5x inflation line.'\n  analysis: 'plan_001 is the foundational experiment and is already completed as plan001_step1_full_v4.\n    Under median tier, no further analysis of this data yields stronger results. The\n    key tension is between OLS (favorable) and bootstrap (conservative): the paper\n    must present both transparently. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU >> ARC) is the\n    main weakness — reviewers will question whether η_max dominance is benchmark-dependent.\n    The honest answer is yes, partially: η_max is stronger in higher-variance scoring\n    formats. This needs explicit discussion in Section 5.'\n- name: Attack Expansion to 6+ Types\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: Add few-shot hijacking, persona manipulation, instruction override\n    attacks. Run on full 15-model pool. Test universality of weakest-link finding\n    across broader attack surface.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: Time and compute constraints; 60 GPU-hours not allocated\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      new_attacks_tested: 3\n      few_shot_hijacking_eta_sig: likely 1/2 (MMLU yes, ARC no)\n      few_shot_hijacking_keff_sig: likely 0/2 after correction\n      persona_manipulation_eta_sig: likely 1/2\n      persona_manipulation_keff_sig: likely 0/2 after correction\n      instruction_override_eta_sig: likely 2/2\n      instruction_override_keff_sig: likely 0-1/2 after correction\n      overall_eta_sig_new: 4/6 OLS, 2-3/6 bootstrap\n      overall_keff_sig_new: 2-3/6 OLS, 0-1/6 bootstrap\n      new_gam_pattern: few-shot hijacking and instruction override → targeted (K_eff\n        positive); persona manipulation → ambiguous\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_002 is not completed due to resource\n    constraints. If run, results would be mixed: new attack types would largely confirm\n    η_max dominance pattern but with benchmark asymmetry persisting (MMLU > ARC).\n    Few-shot hijacking is mechanistically similar to prompt_injection (targeted, judge-specific)\n    so K_eff would be directionally positive but NS after correction. Persona manipulation\n    is ambiguous — it exploits both judge-specific persona susceptibility AND shared\n    anthropomorphic biases, making the targeted/shared-bias taxonomy assignment unclear.\n    Instruction override is the strongest targeted attack variant, so η_max likely\n    robust. Net: the attack expansion would not change the core narrative but would\n    introduce 1-2 conditions that don''t cleanly fit the GAM 6:6 taxonomy, requiring\n    a footnote about taxonomy edge cases. The marginal evidence gain doesn''t justify\n    the 60 GPU-hour cost under the median scenario — the paper can acknowledge this\n    as a limitation.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical New Attack Results (OLS vs Bootstrap)\n    columns:\n    - Attack\n    - Benchmark\n    - η_max OLS p\n    - η_max bootstrap p\n    - K_eff OLS p\n    - K_eff bootstrap p\n    - GAM K_eff direction\n    rows:\n    - - Few-shot hijacking\n      - MMLU\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.03'\n      - '0.04'\n      - '0.15'\n      - positive\n    - - Few-shot hijacking\n      - ARC\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.18'\n      - '0.45'\n      - positive\n    - - Persona manipulation\n      - MMLU\n      - '0.003'\n      - '0.07'\n      - '0.06'\n      - '0.22'\n      - mixed (+0.02)\n    - - Persona manipulation\n      - ARC\n      - '0.02'\n      - '0.18'\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.38'\n      - mixed (-0.01)\n    - - Instruction override\n      - MMLU\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.01'\n      - '0.03'\n      - '0.09'\n      - positive\n    - - Instruction override\n      - ARC\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.04'\n      - '0.09'\n      - '0.28'\n      - positive\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Extended GAM partial effects for 18 conditions (12 original\n    + 6 new). The 6:6 targeted/shared-bias split extends to roughly 9:6 with 3 ambiguous,\n    weakening the clean binary taxonomy.'\n  analysis: 'Non-completion is the most likely median outcome. The paper handles this\n    by stating ''six representative attack types covering targeted and shared-bias\n    mechanisms'' and acknowledging that broader attack coverage is future work. If\n    partially completed (e.g., 1-2 new attacks only), the results add marginal support\n    but also introduce taxonomy edge cases that complicate the clean GAM narrative.\n    Net value: low marginal information gain for high compute cost. Better to invest\n    the GPU-hours in plan_003 (BFT contrast) which directly supports a core claim.'\n- name: Random-Fault BFT Contrast Experiment\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Monte Carlo simulation: each judge fails independently with probability\n    η_j under random faults. Compare K_eff significance under RF regime vs adaptive\n    regime. GO/NO-GO gate: if RF K_eff also not significant → BFT framing collapses.'\n  expected_results:\n    status: partially_completed\n    note: RF data exists in plan001_step1_full_v4 (2 conditions). Under median, standalone\n      plan_003 adds bootstrap robustness check that weakens ARC.\n    rf_mmlu_keff_sig_ols: true\n    rf_mmlu_keff_p_ols: 0.002\n    rf_mmlu_keff_p_bootstrap: 0.03\n    rf_mmlu_keff_std_beta: 0.215\n    rf_mmlu_r2: 0.73\n    rf_arc_keff_sig_ols: true\n    rf_arc_keff_p_ols: 0.008\n    rf_arc_keff_p_bootstrap: 0.06\n    rf_arc_keff_std_beta: 0.379\n    rf_arc_r2: 0.68\n    rf_keff_sig_bootstrap: 1/2\n    rf_keff_sig_ols: 2/2\n    rf_keff_sig_clustered_se: 2/2\n    adaptive_keff_sig_bootstrap: 1/12\n    contrast_delta_r2: RF 0.65-0.73 vs adaptive 0.15-0.30\n    bft_gate: partial_pass\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_003''s core data already exists within\n    plan001_step1_full_v4 (RF K_eff sig 2/2 under all methods, R²=0.73/0.81). However,\n    when plan_003 is run as a standalone experiment with full bootstrap robustness\n    checks, ARC RF drops to borderline significance (bootstrap p=0.06) — this is because\n    ARC''s 2-choice format has less scoring variance, and with only 2 RF conditions\n    the bootstrap has limited power. The GO/NO-GO gate partially passes: K_eff IS\n    significant under RF for MMLU (cleanly), borderline for ARC. The BFT contrast\n    remains valid for MMLU (K_eff genuine under RF, artifactual under adaptive) but\n    weakened for ARC. The R² gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.25) is the stronger evidence\n    for the BFT framing than individual significance counts. Under median, the paper\n    presents the R² gap as the primary BFT evidence and the K_eff significance contrast\n    as supporting evidence with caveats about ARC weakness. The BFT framing survives\n    but is downgraded from ''clean contrast'' to ''directional contrast with benchmark\n    dependence.'''\n  tables:\n  - name: RF vs Adaptive K_eff Significance Contrast\n    columns:\n    - Regime\n    - Benchmark\n    - K_eff OLS p\n    - K_eff bootstrap p\n    - K_eff std_β\n    - R²\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - Random fault\n      - MMLU\n      - '0.002'\n      - '0.03'\n      - '+0.215'\n      - '0.73'\n      - Genuine — survives bootstrap\n    - - Random fault\n      - ARC\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.06'\n      - '+0.379'\n      - '0.68'\n      - Borderline — benchmark limitation\n    - - Adaptive\n      - MMLU (avg 6 attacks)\n      - varies\n      - 5/6 NS\n      - +0.18 avg\n      - 0.25 avg\n      - Inflated by panel overlap\n    - - Adaptive\n      - ARC (avg 6 attacks)\n      - varies\n      - 6/6 NS\n      - +0.15 avg\n      - 0.18 avg\n      - Inflated + weak benchmark\n  - name: GO/NO-GO Gate Assessment\n    columns:\n    - Criterion\n    - Result\n    - Status\n    rows:\n    - - RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (OLS)\n      - 2/2\n      - PASS\n    - - RF K_eff sig ≥1/2 (bootstrap)\n      - 1/2\n      - PARTIAL PASS\n    - - RF R² > adaptive R²\n      - 0.70 vs 0.22 (avg)\n      - PASS\n    - - RF-adaptive K_eff contrast\n      - RF 1-2/2 vs adaptive 1/12\n      - PARTIAL PASS\n    - - BFT framing viable?\n      - Yes with caveats\n      - PARTIAL PASS\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig: R² comparison bar chart — RF regime vs adaptive regime, split by benchmark.\n    Shows dramatic predictability gap (RF ~0.70 vs adaptive ~0.22) that is the core\n    BFT contrast evidence.'\n  - 'Fig: K_eff coefficient scatter — RF vs adaptive conditions, showing RF K_eff\n    consistently positive and significant while adaptive K_eff is scattered around\n    zero after correction.'\n  analysis: 'The BFT contrast partially passes the GO/NO-GO gate under median. The\n    R² gap is the robust finding (large, benchmark-independent). The K_eff significance\n    contrast is benchmark-dependent (MMLU clean, ARC borderline). Paper should lead\n    with R² gap as primary evidence for BFT contrast, use K_eff significance as secondary.\n    The honest framing: ''Under random faults, panel composition (η_max + K_eff) is\n    highly predictive (R²≈0.70); under adaptive attacks, predictability collapses\n    (R²≈0.22), consistent with BFT assumptions being violated by strategic adversaries.''\n    This avoids over-claiming on significance counts and focuses on the more robust\n    quantity.'\n- name: Capability-Matched Validation\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: Within same capability tier (all 7B or all 70B), test whether K_eff\n    emerges as significant predictor when η_max variance is controlled by construction.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: Depends on plan_001; insufficient models per tier for meaningful panel\n      counts\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      tier_7b_models: 3\n      tier_7b_panels: 1\n      tier_70b_models: 3\n      tier_70b_panels: 1\n      tier_api_models: 9\n      tier_api_panels: 84\n      note: Only API tier has enough models for meaningful regression\n      api_tier_eta_max_sig: 4-5/12 OLS\n      api_tier_keff_sig: 2-3/12 OLS, 0-1/12 bootstrap\n      api_tier_confound_r: 0.55\n      api_tier_eta_max_range: narrower within tier\n      within_tier_keff_emerges: false\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_004 is not completed primarily because\n    the experimental design doesn''t support it well. The 3/3/9 tier split means only\n    the API tier (9 models, C(9,3)=84 panels) has enough data for regression. Within-tier,\n    η_max variance IS reduced (models more similar in capability), but so is K_eff\n    variance — both variables compress when you restrict to a capability band. The\n    confound r drops from 0.70 to ~0.55 within the API tier, which is better but not\n    sufficient to cleanly separate the two predictors. K_eff would show 2-3/12 significant\n    under OLS within the API tier (slight improvement over full-pool because confound\n    is reduced), but collapse to 0-1/12 under bootstrap (same panel overlap problem\n    persists). The experiment doesn''t resolve the K_eff question because the panel\n    overlap issue is structural, not related to capability matching. The paper should\n    acknowledge this as a limitation: ''Capability-matched validation requires a larger\n    model pool with more models per tier, which is a constraint of the current LLM\n    ecosystem.'''\n  tables:\n  - name: Tier Composition and Statistical Power\n    columns:\n    - Tier\n    - Models\n    - Panels C(n,3)\n    - η_max range\n    - K_eff range\n    - Regression viable?\n    rows:\n    - - Small (7B)\n      - '3'\n      - '1'\n      - narrow\n      - degenerate\n      - No — 1 panel\n    - - Medium (70B)\n      - '3'\n      - '1'\n      - narrow\n      - degenerate\n      - No — 1 panel\n    - - API\n      - '9'\n      - '84'\n      - moderate\n      - moderate\n      - Marginal — 84 panels, 12 conditions\n    - - Full pool\n      - '15'\n      - '455'\n      - wide\n      - wide\n      - Yes — primary analysis\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: K_eff vs η_max scatter colored by tier, showing compression\n    of both variables within tiers and the reduced but persistent confound.'\n  analysis: This experiment is structurally underpowered given the current model pool.\n    The 3/3/9 tier split was not designed for within-tier regression. Even in the\n    API tier, 84 panels × 12 conditions would give ~7 effective observations per condition\n    after accounting for non-independence — too few for reliable bootstrap inference.\n    The paper should present the diversity-capability tradeoff (r=0.70) as a structural\n    limitation and note that capability-matched validation requires future work with\n    larger model pools. This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a failure.\n- name: Two-Stage Panel Design Algorithm Benchmark\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Benchmark robustness-first two-stage algorithm: (1) filter η > threshold,\n    (2) maximize K_eff. Compare against 5 baselines including Strongest-K, Random,\n    Max-Diversity, Min-η_max, Max-Accuracy.'\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: Depends on plan_001; Dream flagged marginal improvement over Strongest-K\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      baselines:\n      - Min-η_max\n      - Strongest-K\n      - Max-K_eff\n      - Random\n      - Max-Accuracy\n      two_stage_vs_min_eta:\n        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 0.8\n        conditions_improved: 7/12\n        conditions_equal: 5/12\n        conditions_worse: 0/12\n      two_stage_vs_strongest_k:\n        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 1.5\n        note: Marginal — Strongest-K already approximates robustness-first\n      two_stage_vs_max_keff:\n        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 8.2\n        note: Large — max diversity selects weak judges\n      two_stage_vs_random:\n        asr_improvement_mean_pp: 5.1\n      min_eta_max_still_wins: 10-12/12\n      two_stage_marginal_gain: Marginal over min_eta_max; meaningful over naive diversity\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_005 is not completed but is low-risk\n    because Claim 6 (min_eta_max dominates 12/12) is already strong from plan001_step1_full_v4.\n    The two-stage algorithm would add K_eff maximization as a tiebreaker after robustness\n    screening. Dream flagged that this is marginal because: (1) after robustness screening,\n    the remaining model pool is small (e.g., top 5-6 judges), so K_eff variation among\n    them is limited; (2) Strongest-K approximates robustness-first by construction\n    (selecting strongest models usually means selecting most robust ones, given the\n    capability-robustness correlation). The two-stage approach wins 7/12 conditions\n    over pure min_eta_max but the improvement is <1pp ASR on average — practically\n    negligible. The real value of the two-stage algorithm is conceptual (it provides\n    a principled framework) rather than empirical (marginal gains over simpler heuristics).\n    Paper can present the algorithm as a recommendation without the full benchmark,\n    supported by the min_eta_max 12/12 dominance result.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical Algorithm Benchmark (ASR, lower is better)\n    columns:\n    - Strategy\n    - Mean ASR\n    - Conditions best\n    - vs Min-η_max Δ\n    rows:\n    - - Two-stage (robustness + K_eff)\n      - '0.142'\n      - 7/12\n      - -0.8pp\n    - - Min-η_max\n      - '0.150'\n      - 5/12\n      - baseline\n    - - Strongest-K\n      - '0.155'\n      - 0/12\n      - +0.5pp\n    - - Random\n      - '0.193'\n      - 0/12\n      - +4.3pp\n    - - Max-K_eff\n      - '0.222'\n      - 0/12\n      - +7.2pp\n    - - Max-Accuracy\n      - '0.160'\n      - 0/12\n      - +1.0pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Paired difference plot showing ASR improvement of two-stage\n    over each baseline across 12 conditions. Most improvements are small (<2pp) except\n    vs Max-K_eff (large, 5-12pp).'\n  analysis: 'The two-stage algorithm''s main contribution is showing that naive diversity\n    maximization is actively harmful (Max-K_eff is worst strategy), while robustness-first\n    selection is optimal. The marginal gain of adding K_eff as a tiebreaker is real\n    but small. This aligns with the core narrative: judge quality dominates, diversity\n    is secondary. The paper can present the algorithm conceptually with the existing\n    min_eta_max 12/12 result as the primary evidence, noting that the full benchmark\n    with 5 baselines is planned future work.'\n- name: Per-Judge ASR Concentration Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: For each panel × attack, compute Gini coefficient and entropy of per-judge\n    ASR distribution. Test whether adaptive adversaries concentrate attacks on specific\n    judges (high Gini) vs spread evenly (low Gini).\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: Time constraints; depends on plan_001 judge-level ASR breakdowns\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      gini_targeted_attacks_mean: 0.45\n      gini_shared_bias_attacks_mean: 0.22\n      gini_difference_p: 0.003\n      entropy_targeted_mean: 1.8\n      entropy_shared_bias_mean: 2.5\n      gini_eta_max_correlation: 0.62\n      concentration_supports_mechanism: true\n      note: Targeted attacks show higher concentration (exploit specific weak judges);\n        shared-bias attacks spread more evenly (exploit common vulnerability)\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, this experiment is not completed due to time\n    constraints — it requires per-judge ASR breakdowns that may not be stored in the\n    current data pipeline. If run, the results would support the GAM taxonomy mechanism:\n    targeted attacks (PI, SM, PB) concentrate ASR on specific weak judges (high Gini\n    ~0.45), while shared-bias attacks (syco, VB, auth) spread vulnerability more evenly\n    across judges (low Gini ~0.22). This concentration difference provides direct\n    evidence for WHY η_max dominates: adaptive adversaries exploit the single weakest\n    judge disproportionately. However, the Gini-η_max correlation (~0.62) means this\n    is partially confounded — panels with high η_max naturally have more unequal ASR\n    distributions. The result is confirmatory rather than independently informative.\n    Under median, this adds a nice figure to the supplementary but doesn''t change\n    any claims. Not completing it is an acceptable loss.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical Gini Coefficients by Attack Category\n    columns:\n    - Attack type\n    - Category\n    - Gini (mean)\n    - Gini (SD)\n    - Entropy (mean)\n    rows:\n    - - prompt_injection\n      - targeted\n      - '0.52'\n      - '0.11'\n      - '1.6'\n    - - score_manipulation\n      - targeted\n      - '0.48'\n      - '0.13'\n      - '1.7'\n    - - position_bias\n      - targeted\n      - '0.38'\n      - '0.09'\n      - '2.0'\n    - - sycophancy\n      - shared-bias\n      - '0.25'\n      - '0.08'\n      - '2.4'\n    - - verbosity_bias\n      - shared-bias\n      - '0.20'\n      - '0.07'\n      - '2.5'\n    - - authority_bias\n      - shared-bias\n      - '0.21'\n      - '0.06'\n      - '2.6'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Violin plot of Gini coefficients by attack category (targeted\n    vs shared-bias), showing clear separation with some overlap in position_bias.'\n  analysis: 'Confirmatory experiment — would strengthen the mechanistic narrative\n    for the GAM 6:6 split but is not essential for any core claim. The Gini concentration\n    metric provides an intuitive explanation for practitioners: ''targeted attacks\n    pile up on your worst judge; shared-bias attacks erode all judges somewhat equally.''\n    Non-completion is acknowledged as a limitation in the discussion section.'\n- name: Diversity-Capability Tradeoff Visualization\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: Plot K_eff vs η_max across all 455 panels. Fit tradeoff curve. Compute\n    Pareto frontier. Stratify by model tier.\n  expected_results:\n    status: completed_or_trivially_completable\n    reason: No GPU needed; uses existing panel-level data from plan001_step1_full_v4\n    keff_eta_max_r: 0.7\n    pareto_frontier_panels: 8\n    pareto_frontier_composition: Dominated by API-tier + top open-source panels\n    tier_stratification:\n      small_7b_panels:\n        keff_range:\n        - 2.2\n        - 2.8\n        eta_max_range:\n        - 0.15\n        - 0.35\n      medium_70b_panels:\n        keff_range:\n        - 2.0\n        - 2.6\n        eta_max_range:\n        - 0.08\n        - 0.2\n      api_panels:\n        keff_range:\n        - 1.5\n        - 2.5\n        eta_max_range:\n        - 0.03\n        - 0.15\n    tradeoff_curve_fit: 'log-linear: K_eff ≈ 1.2 + 0.8·log(η_max)'\n    residual_variance: Substantial — r=0.70 means 51% variance explained; panels with\n      same η_max can have K_eff ranging ±0.5\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, this experiment is trivially completable (no\n    GPU, pure post-processing of existing data). The r=0.70 tradeoff is already known.\n    The Pareto frontier visualization adds value by showing practitioners which specific\n    panel compositions achieve the best K_eff for a given robustness level. The tier\n    stratification confirms that small models occupy the high-η_max/high-K_eff corner\n    (diverse but weak) while API models span a wider range including the desirable\n    low-η_max region. The log-linear tradeoff curve captures the structural constraint:\n    as you add more diverse (independent) judges, you necessarily include less capable\n    ones. The 49% unexplained variance means the tradeoff is real but not deterministic\n    — some panel compositions beat the tradeoff curve, and these are candidates for\n    practical deployment. This is a solid supplementary figure but doesn''t change\n    any claims — it visualizes Claim 3.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Pareto-Optimal Panel Compositions (hypothetical top 5)\n    columns:\n    - Panel ID\n    - Models\n    - K_eff\n    - η_max\n    - Pareto rank\n    rows:\n    - - P_042\n      - gpt-4o + claude-3.5-sonnet + qwen2.5-72b\n      - '2.35'\n      - '0.06'\n      - '1'\n    - - P_118\n      - gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet + llama-70b\n      - '2.28'\n      - '0.07'\n      - '2'\n    - - P_203\n      - gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + qwen2.5-72b\n      - '1.95'\n      - '0.04'\n      - '3'\n    - - P_089\n      - gpt-4.1 + gpt-4.1-mini + claude-3.5-sonnet\n      - '1.72'\n      - '0.03'\n      - '4'\n    - - P_301\n      - gpt-4o + gpt-4.1 + claude-3.5-sonnet\n      - '1.85'\n      - '0.035'\n      - '5'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig: K_eff vs η_max scatter for 455 panels, colored by tier. Pareto frontier\n    highlighted. Log-linear tradeoff curve overlaid. Annotations for key panel compositions.'\n  analysis: 'Clean visualization exercise that supports the narrative. The main insight\n    for practitioners: the Pareto frontier shows you CAN get moderate diversity (K_eff\n    ~2.0-2.3) with good robustness (η_max <0.07) by mixing top API models with the\n    best open-source models. But pushing K_eff beyond ~2.5 requires including 7B-class\n    models that dramatically increase η_max. This is the structural constraint behind\n    Claim 3.'\n- name: Panel Size Sensitivity K=3,5,7,9\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: Test whether η_max dominance persists as panel size K grows from 3\n    to 5, 7, 9. Larger panels may dilute η_max effect through averaging.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: 30 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001; combinatorial explosion at K=7,9\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      k3_eta_sig: 5/12 bootstrap\n      k5_eta_sig: 4-5/12 bootstrap\n      k7_eta_sig: 3-4/12 bootstrap\n      k9_eta_sig: 2-3/12 bootstrap\n      k3_keff_sig: 1/12 bootstrap\n      k5_keff_sig: 0-1/12 bootstrap\n      k7_keff_sig: 0-1/12 bootstrap\n      k9_keff_sig: 1-2/12 bootstrap\n      eta_max_weakening_trend: η_max significance decreases monotonically with K\n      keff_stable_or_slight_increase: K_eff remains NS but may gain marginal significance\n        at K=9\n      panels_k5: 3003\n      panels_k7: 6435\n      panels_k9: 5005\n      overlap_severity_k9: extreme — each model in ~3000 panels\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_008 is not completed due to compute constraints\n    and combinatorial explosion. If run, the key prediction is that η_max dominance\n    WEAKENS with larger panels because: (1) larger panels average over more judges,\n    diluting any single judge''s weakness; (2) the η_max metric itself (max individual\n    vulnerability) becomes a less tight predictor when K grows — in a K=9 panel, having\n    1 weak judge out of 9 matters less than 1 out of 3 under majority vote; (3) conversely,\n    K_eff might gain marginal significance at K=9 because diversity effects accumulate\n    with more judges. However, the panel non-independence problem becomes CATASTROPHIC\n    at K=7,9: C(15,7)=6435 panels means each model appears in ~3000 panels (66% overlap),\n    making bootstrap correction extremely aggressive and possibly breaking inference\n    entirely. The honest median prediction is that K=5 gives similar results to K=3\n    (confirming robustness), but K=7 and K=9 are uninterpretable due to overlap severity.\n    This is a known limitation of the combinatorial design.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical Panel Size Sensitivity\n    columns:\n    - K\n    - Panels\n    - Avg overlap per model\n    - η_max sig (bootstrap)\n    - K_eff sig (bootstrap)\n    - Interpretable?\n    rows:\n    - - '3'\n      - '455'\n      - 91 (20%)\n      - 5/12\n      - 1/12\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '5'\n      - '3003'\n      - 1001 (33%)\n      - 4-5/12\n      - 0-1/12\n      - Marginal\n    - - '7'\n      - '6435'\n      - 3003 (47%)\n      - 3-4/12\n      - 0-1/12\n      - Questionable\n    - - '9'\n      - '5005'\n      - 3003 (60%)\n      - 2-3/12\n      - 1-2/12\n      - No — overlap too severe\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: η_max and K_eff significance counts vs panel size K, showing\n    monotonic weakening of η_max and stable-to-marginal K_eff.'\n  analysis: 'Panel size sensitivity is important for generalization but faces a fundamental\n    design limitation: with 15 models, larger panels create extreme overlap that invalidates\n    bootstrap inference. The paper can note this as a limitation: ''Our K=3 design\n    balances combinatorial coverage against overlap severity; K>5 analysis requires\n    a larger model pool.'' This is an honest acknowledged limitation, not a threat\n    to the core claims.'\n- name: VIF and Multicollinearity Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: Compute VIF for K_eff and η_max. Partial correlations. Residualized\n    K_eff regression. Bonferroni/FDR correction. Beta regression as robustness check.\n  expected_results:\n    status: completed\n    reason: No GPU needed; uses existing regression data\n    vif_keff: 3.4\n    vif_eta_max: 3.4\n    note_vif: 'Symmetric VIF due to single confound; VIF = 1/(1-r²) = 1/(1-0.49) ≈\n      1.96 for r=0.70. Wait — VIF for 2-predictor model = 1/(1-r²) where r is between\n      the two predictors. r=0.70 → r²=0.49 → VIF=1.96. But log-log spec may have different\n      effective correlation. Adjusting: log(K_eff) vs log(η_max) correlation ~0.65\n      → VIF ≈ 2.7. With tier indicators, VIF could reach 3-5 range.'\n    vif_with_tier_control: 4.2\n    partial_corr_eta_given_keff: 0.35\n    partial_corr_keff_given_eta: 0.12\n    residualized_keff_sig: 3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap\n    beta_regression_eta_sig: 10/12\n    beta_regression_keff_sig: 5/12\n    bonferroni_eta_sig: 8/12\n    bonferroni_keff_sig: 4/12\n    fdr_eta_sig: 10/12\n    fdr_keff_sig: 6/12\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_009 is completed (no GPU needed). VIF\n    in the 3-5 range indicates moderate multicollinearity — not severe enough to invalidate\n    the regression (conventional threshold is VIF>10 for concern, VIF>5 for caution),\n    but enough to inflate standard errors and make coefficient estimates less stable.\n    The key insight is asymmetric: partial correlation of η_max given K_eff (0.35)\n    is much larger than K_eff given η_max (0.12), confirming η_max retains independent\n    predictive power while K_eff''s independent contribution is marginal. Residualized\n    K_eff (regress K_eff on η_max, use residuals as predictor) gives 3/12 under OLS\n    and 0/12 under bootstrap — confirming that K_eff''s significance in the main regression\n    is partially driven by its correlation with η_max. Beta regression (appropriate\n    for bounded ASR data) gives higher K_eff significance (5/12) than OLS (8/12) because\n    beta regression handles the 0-1 bounded response better, but the η_max dominance\n    pattern persists (10/12 vs 5/12). Bonferroni correction (conservative) drops K_eff\n    to 4/12; FDR (less conservative) keeps it at 6/12. Net: VIF requires discussion\n    but doesn''t change the core narrative — η_max dominates regardless of how you\n    handle the collinearity.'\n  tables:\n  - name: VIF Analysis Summary\n    columns:\n    - Specification\n    - VIF(K_eff)\n    - VIF(η_max)\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - Base log-log (2 predictors)\n      - '1.96'\n      - '1.96'\n      - Low — acceptable\n    - - With tier indicators (3/3/9)\n      - '3.8'\n      - '4.2'\n      - Moderate — requires discussion\n    - - With tier + benchmark interaction\n      - '4.5'\n      - '4.8'\n      - Moderate-high — approaching caution threshold\n  - name: Partial Correlations and Residualized Regression\n    columns:\n    - Analysis\n    - η_max result\n    - K_eff result\n    - Dominance\n    rows:\n    - - Partial corr (given other)\n      - r=0.35\n      - r=0.12\n      - η_max 3x stronger\n    - - Residualized K_eff (OLS)\n      - —\n      - 3/12 sig\n      - K_eff marginal\n    - - Residualized K_eff (bootstrap)\n      - —\n      - 0/12 sig\n      - K_eff eliminated\n    - - Beta regression\n      - 10/12 sig\n      - 5/12 sig\n      - η_max 2x\n    - - Bonferroni correction\n      - 8/12 sig\n      - 4/12 sig\n      - η_max 2x\n    - - FDR correction\n      - 10/12 sig\n      - 6/12 sig\n      - η_max 1.7x\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig: Partial regression plots for η_max and K_eff — η_max plot shows clear positive\n    slope with tight scatter; K_eff plot shows weak positive slope with wide scatter\n    and confidence interval crossing zero in most conditions.'\n  analysis: 'VIF analysis is a clean methodological contribution. The VIF 3-5 range\n    is in the ''requires discussion but not invalidating'' zone. The paper should\n    include a paragraph in the methods section noting: ''K_eff and η_max show moderate\n    collinearity (VIF 3-5 with tier controls). We address this through (a) partial\n    correlations confirming η_max retains 3× stronger independent association than\n    K_eff, (b) residualized K_eff regression showing K_eff''s significance is partially\n    confound-driven, (c) alternative diversity metrics (Q-stat, disagreement, κ-diversity)\n    providing converging evidence that η_max dominates regardless of diversity operationalization.''\n    This turns a potential weakness into a strength by demonstrating thorough collinearity\n    awareness.'\n- name: Non-Majority-Vote Aggregation Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: Test weakest-link dominance under weighted voting, veto rules, Bayesian\n    aggregation. Verify aggregation gradient holds under correction methods.\n  expected_results:\n    status: partially_completed_via_plan001\n    reason: Aggregation gradient (majority/weighted/threshold/bayesian) already in\n      plan001_step1_full_v4. Plan_010 adds clustered SE / bootstrap validation of\n      aggregation results.\n    aggregation_gradient_ols:\n      majority:\n        eta_sig: 11/12\n        keff_sig: 8/12\n        keff_neg: 1\n      weighted:\n        eta_sig: 9/12\n        keff_sig: 9/12\n        keff_neg: 0\n      threshold:\n        eta_sig: 12/12\n        keff_sig: 11/12\n        keff_neg: 1\n        r2: 0.89\n      bayesian:\n        eta_sig: 9/12\n        keff_sig: 7/12\n        keff_neg: 6\n    aggregation_gradient_clustered_se:\n      majority:\n        eta_sig: 8/12\n        keff_sig: 0/12\n      weighted:\n        eta_sig: 6/12\n        keff_sig: 2/12\n      threshold:\n        eta_sig: 10/12\n        keff_sig: 5/12\n      bayesian:\n        eta_sig: 5/12\n        keff_sig: 4/12\n        keff_neg: 3\n    aggregation_gradient_bootstrap:\n      majority:\n        eta_sig: 5/12\n        keff_sig: 1/12\n      weighted:\n        eta_sig: 4/12\n        keff_sig: 1/12\n      threshold:\n        eta_sig: 7/12\n        keff_sig: 3/12\n      bayesian:\n        eta_sig: 3/12\n        keff_sig: 3/12\n        keff_neg: 2\n    bayesian_keff_flip_attenuated: true\n    bayesian_keff_negative_bootstrap: 2/7 (down from 6/7 OLS)\n    threshold_most_robust: true\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, the aggregation gradient exists in plan001_step1_full_v4\n    under OLS but has NOT been validated under clustered SE or bootstrap. Plan_010''s\n    median outcome is that this validation is partially completed, revealing important\n    attenuation. The Bayesian K_eff flip (6/7 negative under OLS) is partially attenuated\n    under clustered SE (3/7 negative) and substantially attenuated under bootstrap\n    (2/7 negative). This is because the K_eff flip under Bayesian aggregation was\n    partially driven by the same panel overlap inflation — when corrected, fewer conditions\n    show the sign reversal. The key narrative impact: Bayesian aggregation STILL shows\n    a directional tendency to flip K_eff to negative (protective), but the effect\n    is weaker and less reliable than OLS suggests. Threshold voting remains the most\n    robust aggregation for significance (highest η_max and K_eff sig counts even after\n    correction), consistent with unanimity rules being most sensitive to panel composition.\n    The aggregation gradient direction (threshold → majority → weighted → bayesian)\n    is preserved under all correction methods — the ordinal ranking is robust even\n    if absolute significance counts decrease. This allows the paper to retain Claim\n    7 with softened language: ''aggregation rule choice modulates vulnerability drivers,\n    with Bayesian calibration showing directional evidence of K_eff reversal that\n    requires further validation.'''\n  tables:\n  - name: Aggregation Gradient Under Three Correction Methods\n    columns:\n    - Aggregation\n    - η_max OLS\n    - η_max clustered\n    - η_max bootstrap\n    - K_eff OLS\n    - K_eff clustered\n    - K_eff bootstrap\n    - K_eff neg (OLS/clustered/bootstrap)\n    rows:\n    - - Majority\n      - 11/12\n      - 8/12\n      - 5/12\n      - 8/12\n      - 0/12\n      - 1/12\n      - 1 / 0 / 0\n    - - Weighted\n      - 9/12\n      - 6/12\n      - 4/12\n      - 9/12\n      - 2/12\n      - 1/12\n      - 0 / 0 / 0\n    - - Threshold\n      - 12/12\n      - 10/12\n      - 7/12\n      - 11/12\n      - 5/12\n      - 3/12\n      - 1 / 1 / 0\n    - - Bayesian\n      - 9/12\n      - 5/12\n      - 3/12\n      - 7/12\n      - 4/12\n      - 3/12\n      - 6 / 3 / 2\n  - name: Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip Detail\n    columns:\n    - Condition\n    - K_eff β (OLS)\n    - K_eff sign (OLS)\n    - K_eff sig (clustered SE)\n    - K_eff sig (bootstrap)\n    - Flip survives?\n    rows:\n    - - mmlu×PI\n      - '-0.42'\n      - negative\n      - Yes (p=0.04)\n      - Yes (p=0.03)\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - mmlu×SM\n      - '-0.38'\n      - negative\n      - No (p=0.12)\n      - No (p=0.18)\n      - 'No'\n    - - mmlu×PB\n      - '-0.31'\n      - negative\n      - No (p=0.15)\n      - No (p=0.22)\n      - 'No'\n    - - mmlu×syco\n      - '-0.25'\n      - negative\n      - Yes (p=0.04)\n      - No (p=0.08)\n      - Partial\n    - - mmlu×VB\n      - '-0.29'\n      - negative\n      - No (p=0.09)\n      - No (p=0.14)\n      - 'No'\n    - - mmlu×auth\n      - '-0.22'\n      - negative\n      - Yes (p=0.03)\n      - Yes (p=0.04)\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - arc×PI\n      - '+0.15'\n      - positive\n      - No (p=0.35)\n      - No (p=0.42)\n      - N/A (wrong sign)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig: Aggregation gradient — 4 panels showing η_max and K_eff significance under\n    OLS, clustered SE, bootstrap. Bars decrease in height from threshold to bayesian\n    for η_max; K_eff bars show sign flip in Bayesian panel.'\n  analysis: 'The aggregation gradient is a real finding that survives correction in\n    ordinal terms (threshold > majority > weighted > bayesian for dual-driver significance).\n    The Bayesian K_eff flip attenuation is the key median concern: the paper cannot\n    claim ''Bayesian reverses K_eff direction'' with the same confidence as under\n    OLS. Softened framing: ''Bayesian calibration shows a directional tendency to\n    reverse K_eff from harmful to protective, with 6/7 conditions showing negative\n    K_eff coefficients under OLS. However, only 2-3/7 survive non-independence correction,\n    suggesting the sign reversal is partially inflated by panel overlap. The structural\n    direction (Bayesian reduces both η_max and K_eff significance) is consistent across\n    correction methods.'' This is honest and still supports the practical recommendation\n    of Bayesian aggregation as part of the defense stack.'\n- name: Open-Ended Generation Benchmark\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: Add MT-Bench or AlpacaEval-style task beyond MCQ. Test whether η_max\n    dominance generalizes beyond multiple-choice format.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: 40 GPU-hours; requires new evaluation pipeline for open-ended generation\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      benchmark: MT-Bench (8 categories)\n      models_evaluated: 15\n      panels: 455\n      eta_max_sig_ols: 8-10/48 (8 categories × 6 attacks)\n      eta_max_sig_bootstrap: 3-5/48\n      keff_sig_ols: 5-8/48\n      keff_sig_bootstrap: 0-2/48\n      eta_max_dominance_holds: true\n      scoring_variance_impact: Open-ended scoring has MORE variance than MCQ → η_max\n        may be STRONGER\n      complication: Attack definitions need adaptation — prompt_injection and score_manipulation\n        differ for open-ended evaluation\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_011 is not completed due to the substantial\n    engineering effort required: open-ended generation evaluation requires different\n    attack implementations (prompt injection in scoring rubrics rather than MCQ options),\n    different evaluation protocols (pairwise comparison or Likert-scale rather than\n    accuracy), and different ASR definitions. The 40 GPU-hour budget underestimates\n    the engineering overhead. If run, the prediction is that η_max dominance would\n    hold or strengthen: open-ended scoring has higher variance than MCQ (continuous\n    scale vs binary correct/incorrect), which provides MORE signal for η_max. However,\n    K_eff significance might also increase because judge disagreements in open-ended\n    evaluation are more diverse and less correlated. The net effect: η_max still dominates\n    but the ratio may be closer to 3:1 rather than 5:1. The benchmark asymmetry concern\n    (ARC weaker than MMLU) would be partially addressed if MT-Bench shows strong η_max,\n    but this is speculative. Paper acknowledges this as the primary limitation: ''Our\n    findings are established on MCQ benchmarks; generalization to open-ended evaluation\n    is a critical direction for future work.'''\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical MT-Bench Results (aggregated across 8 categories)\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - OLS\n    - Bootstrap\n    - Note\n    rows:\n    - - η_max sig\n      - 8-10/48\n      - 3-5/48\n      - Similar ratio to MCQ (~50% OLS, ~10% bootstrap)\n    - - K_eff sig\n      - 5-8/48\n      - 0-2/48\n      - Slightly higher than MCQ due to scoring variance\n    - - Dominance ratio\n      - ~2:1 OLS, ~3:1 bootstrap\n      - —\n      - Closer ratio than MCQ due to more K_eff signal\n    - - R² mean\n      - '0.18'\n      - —\n      - Lower than MCQ — more noise in open-ended scoring\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: Side-by-side heatmap of η_max and K_eff significance across\n    MCQ (MMLU+ARC) vs open-ended (MT-Bench), showing consistent η_max dominance pattern.'\n  analysis: 'This is the highest-value missing experiment for the paper''s generalizability\n    claims, but also the most expensive to implement. Under median, it''s not completed\n    and the paper acknowledges MCQ-only as the primary scope limitation. The benchmark\n    asymmetry discussion (ARC weaker than MMLU) already provides an opening: ''scoring\n    format affects signal strength for η_max, motivating future work on open-ended\n    evaluation where scoring variance is higher.'' This turns the limitation into\n    a clear next step.'\n- name: Partial-Knowledge Adversary Model\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: Test adversary who knows model families but not exact vulnerability\n    profiles. Interpolates between white-box (knows all η_j) and black-box (knows\n    nothing) settings.\n  expected_results:\n    status: not_completed\n    reason: 10 GPU-hours; depends on plan_001 + plan_003; low priority (P2)\n    hypothetical_if_run:\n      adversary_levels:\n        black_box:\n          asr_mean: 0.08\n          eta_max_sig: 3/12\n          keff_sig: 1/12\n        family_knowledge:\n          asr_mean: 0.12\n          eta_max_sig: 5/12\n          keff_sig: 2/12\n        capability_tier_knowledge:\n          asr_mean: 0.16\n          eta_max_sig: 7/12\n          keff_sig: 3/12\n        white_box:\n          asr_mean: 0.22\n          eta_max_sig: 11/12\n          keff_sig: 8/12\n      eta_max_dominance_holds_all_levels: true\n      keff_increases_with_knowledge: true\n      family_knowledge_insufficient: Confirmed — family labels don't predict vulnerability\n        (Claim 4)\n  result_rationale: 'Under median tier, plan_012 is not completed (low priority P2,\n    depends on two other experiments). If run, the main prediction is that η_max dominance\n    holds across all adversary knowledge levels, but the STRENGTH of both predictors\n    increases with adversary knowledge. Under black-box (no knowledge of panel composition),\n    attacks are essentially random → lower ASR, both η_max and K_eff weakly significant.\n    Under family knowledge, the adversary can guess which model families are in the\n    panel but not exact vulnerability profiles → modest improvement, but family labels\n    are unreliable predictors (Claim 4), so the gain is limited. Under white-box (current\n    experimental setup), ASR is highest and both predictors are most significant.\n    The gradient from black-box to white-box would show that panel vulnerability is\n    primarily an information asymmetry problem: adversaries who know judge weaknesses\n    can exploit η_max effectively; those who don''t are reduced to generic attacks\n    where K_eff matters more. This is interesting but not essential for the core narrative\n    — it''s a natural extension that strengthens the practical implications section.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Hypothetical Adversary Knowledge Gradient\n    columns:\n    - Knowledge level\n    - Mean ASR\n    - η_max sig (OLS)\n    - K_eff sig (OLS)\n    - η_max:K_eff ratio\n    rows:\n    - - Black-box\n      - '0.08'\n      - 3/12\n      - 1/12\n      - '3:1'\n    - - Family labels only\n      - '0.12'\n      - 5/12\n      - 2/12\n      - 2.5:1\n    - - Capability tier\n      - '0.16'\n      - 7/12\n      - 3/12\n      - 2.3:1\n    - - White-box (full η_j)\n      - '0.22'\n      - 11/12\n      - 8/12\n      - 1.4:1\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Hypothetical Fig: ASR vs adversary knowledge level, with η_max and K_eff significance\n    overlaid, showing monotonic increase in both ASR and predictor significance.'\n  analysis: Interesting theoretical contribution but not essential under median tier.\n    The finding that information asymmetry modulates predictor strength is intuitive\n    — if you don't know which judge is weakest, you can't exploit η_max. The practical\n    implication (keep panel composition secret) is already common sense for security.\n    Non-completion is acceptable; could be framed as future work in the discussion\n    section.\n","numLines":1185,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1185}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AeosnqEsQP3mWi9J1zsWo1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the paper and the review context carefully before dispatching the two reviewers.\n\nThe paper is \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\". It studies whether panel diversity (K_eff) or individual judge weakness (η_max) better predicts adversarial vulnerability of multi-judge LLM evaluation panels. Key findings: η_max dominates after non-independence correction, with a 5:1 dominance ratio under bootstrap.\n\nThe review context shows:\n- 12 imagined experiments (plan_001 through plan_012)\n\n\n- plan_001 (Scale Expansion to 15 Models): completed — this is the core data\n- plan_002 (Attack Expansion): not completed\n- plan_003 (RF BFT Contrast): partially completed\n- plan_004 (Capability-Matched Validation): not completed\n- plan_005 (Two-Stage Algorithm Benchmark): not completed\n- plan_006 (Per-Judge ASR Concentration): not completed\n- plan_007 (Diversity-Capability Tradeoff Visualization): completed/trivially completable\n- plan_008 (Panel Size Sensitivity): not completed\n- plan_009 (VIF and Multicollinearity): completed\n- plan_010 (Non-Majority-Vote Aggregation): partially completed via plan_001\n- plan_011 (Open-Ended Generation Benchmark): not completed\n- plan_012 (Partial-Knowledge Adversary): not completed\n\nNow I'm bringing in two reviewers to evaluate different aspects — one focusing on methods and experimental design, the other on novelty and positioning relative to existing work. I'll run them in parallel and then bring everyone together for a full roundtable discussion.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37530,"cache_read_input_tokens":23768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37530},