{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AeosnqEsQP3mWi9J1zsWo1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main venue accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a score of 4 means top ~20%.\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\"\n\nThe paper studies whether panel vulnerability in multi-judge LLM evaluation is driven by individual judge weakness (η_max) or structural diversity (K_eff). Using 15 models, 455 three-judge panels, 6 attack types, and 2 benchmarks (12 conditions), they find η_max dominates under non-independence correction (5-8/12 conditions significant) while K_eff collapses (0-3/12). They present methodological contributions around non-independence correction for combinatorial designs, GAM analysis showing a 6:6 targeted/shared-bias taxonomic split, RF vs adaptive BFT contrast, aggregation gradient analysis, and practical min(η_max) selection recommendation.\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages + appendices)\n\n**Section 1 (Intro):** Frames the problem as testing whether BFT diversity assumptions hold for LLM judge panels under adversarial attacks. 15 models, 6 families, 3 capability tiers, C(15,3)=455 panels, 6 attacks, 2 benchmarks. Three contributions claimed: (1) η_max dominance with benchmark-dependent strength, (2) methodological transparency on non-independence, (3) practical defense recommendations.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work):** Four short paragraphs: LLM-as-Judge, Adversarial Attacks on LLM Judges, BFT, Panel Diversity in Evaluation. Very sparse — no specific citations beyond Zheng et al. 2023.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- 3.1: Panel construction — N=15, 6 families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta, Qwen, Mistral), 3 tiers (small/7B, medium/70B, API-frontier). η_max = max individual ASR. K_eff from eigenspectrum of MI matrix.\n- 3.2: 6 attacks in 2 categories: targeted (PI, SM, PB) and shared-bias (syco, VB, auth). 2 benchmarks (MMLU 4-choice, ARC 2-choice).\n- 3.3: Log-log OLS regression: log(ASR+ε) = β0 + β1·log(K_eff) + β2·log(η_max+ε) + ε\n- 3.4: Four correction methods for non-independence: naïve OLS, wild cluster bootstrap (B=9999, Rademacher, 3-position max-p), clustered SE (CR1, 15 clusters), mixed effects (random judge intercepts).\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- 4.1 Main Results: Table 1 — OLS: η_max 11/12, K_eff 8/12; Bootstrap: 5/12, 1/12 (5:1 ratio); Clustered SE: 8/12, 0/12; Mixed effects: 7/12, 3/12. Benchmark asymmetry: MMLU 4/6 bootstrap η_max, ARC 1/6.\n- 4.2 SE Inflation: ~2.5× inflation, each judge in 91/455 panels.\n- 4.3 Multicollinearity: VIF 1.96-4.8 (Table 2). Partial correlations: η_max|K_eff = 0.35 vs K_eff|η_max = 0.12. Residualized K_eff: 3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap. Beta regression: η_max 10/12, K_eff 5/12 (Table 3).\n- 4.4 GAM: 6:6 split — targeted positive, shared-bias negative for K_eff. Acknowledged as \"suggestive.\"\n- 4.5 RF BFT Contrast: Table 4 — RF: K_eff p=0.03 (MMLU), 0.06 (ARC), R²≈0.70. Adaptive: K_eff mostly NS, R²≈0.22. GO/NO-GO: partial pass.\n- 4.6 Aggregation Gradient: Table 5 — threshold > majority > weighted > Bayesian for vulnerability. Ordinal ranking preserved across corrections.\n- 4.7 Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip: Table 6 — only 2/7 negative coefficients survive bootstrap (MMLU×PI and MMLU×auth).\n- 4.8 Practical Panel Design: min(η_max) dominates 12/12. K_eff IQR effect = 1.3pp. Family labels don't predict diversity.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** When diversity may help, practical implications, broader applicability.\n\n**Section 5.1 (Limitations):** Benchmark scope (MCQ only), attack coverage (6 types), panel size (K=3 only), capability-matched validation (r=0.70 confound), GAM validation (no bootstrap CIs).\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Restates main findings.\n\n**Appendix A:** Diversity-capability tradeoff visualization (K_eff vs η_max, log-linear fit, Pareto frontier, tier stratification).\n\n**Appendix B:** GO/NO-GO gate for BFT framing (Table 7).\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe full experiment design had 12 planned experiments. The paper covers:\n- plan_001 (15-model scale expansion): COMPLETED — core data, Tables 1-3\n- plan_003 (RF BFT contrast): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Table 4, borderline ARC\n- plan_007 (diversity-capability tradeoff): COMPLETED — Appendix A\n- plan_009 (VIF/multicollinearity): COMPLETED — Tables 2-3\n- plan_010 (aggregation gradient): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Tables 5-6, OLS gradient exists but clustered SE/bootstrap validation partially done\n\nNOT completed: plan_002 (attack expansion), plan_004 (capability-matched), plan_005 (two-stage algorithm benchmark), plan_006 (per-judge ASR concentration/Gini), plan_008 (panel size K=3,5,7,9), plan_011 (open-ended generation), plan_012 (partial-knowledge adversary).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Cover:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology**: Is the experimental design valid? Are the correction methods appropriate? Is the regression specification well-justified?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the claims supported by the evidence? Are there gaps in the statistical analysis?\n3. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? What critical experiments are missing?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the study?\n5. **Presentation quality**: Are the results clearly presented?\n\nProvide scores for Soundness, Excitement, Reproducibility (each 1-5), and an Overall Assessment (using the ARR scale above). List specific strengths and weaknesses. Be rigorous but fair.\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X (with label)\n\n### Confidence\nX/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37530,"cache_read_input_tokens":23768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37530},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"3830a54f-b28a-4afa-ad3e-b66e75c1ba3e"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a401f18bbf9cfcde7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main venue accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a score of 4 means top ~20%.\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\"\n\nThe paper studies whether panel vulnerability in multi-judge LLM evaluation is driven by individual judge weakness (η_max) or structural diversity (K_eff). Using 15 models, 455 three-judge panels, 6 attack types, and 2 benchmarks (12 conditions), they find η_max dominates under non-independence correction (5-8/12 conditions significant) while K_eff collapses (0-3/12). They present methodological contributions around non-independence correction for combinatorial designs, GAM analysis showing a 6:6 targeted/shared-bias taxonomic split, RF vs adaptive BFT contrast, aggregation gradient analysis, and practical min(η_max) selection recommendation.\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages + appendices)\n\n**Section 1 (Intro):** Frames the problem as testing whether BFT diversity assumptions hold for LLM judge panels under adversarial attacks. 15 models, 6 families, 3 capability tiers, C(15,3)=455 panels, 6 attacks, 2 benchmarks. Three contributions claimed: (1) η_max dominance with benchmark-dependent strength, (2) methodological transparency on non-independence, (3) practical defense recommendations.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work):** Four short paragraphs: LLM-as-Judge, Adversarial Attacks on LLM Judges, BFT, Panel Diversity in Evaluation. Very sparse — no specific citations beyond Zheng et al. 2023.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- 3.1: Panel construction — N=15, 6 families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta, Qwen, Mistral), 3 tiers (small/7B, medium/70B, API-frontier). η_max = max individual ASR. K_eff from eigenspectrum of MI matrix.\n- 3.2: 6 attacks in 2 categories: targeted (PI, SM, PB) and shared-bias (syco, VB, auth). 2 benchmarks (MMLU 4-choice, ARC 2-choice).\n- 3.3: Log-log OLS regression: log(ASR+ε) = β0 + β1·log(K_eff) + β2·log(η_max+ε) + ε\n- 3.4: Four correction methods for non-independence: naïve OLS, wild cluster bootstrap (B=9999, Rademacher, 3-position max-p), clustered SE (CR1, 15 clusters), mixed effects (random judge intercepts).\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- 4.1 Main Results: Table 1 — OLS: η_max 11/12, K_eff 8/12; Bootstrap: 5/12, 1/12 (5:1 ratio); Clustered SE: 8/12, 0/12; Mixed effects: 7/12, 3/12. Benchmark asymmetry: MMLU 4/6 bootstrap η_max, ARC 1/6.\n- 4.2 SE Inflation: ~2.5× inflation, each judge in 91/455 panels.\n- 4.3 Multicollinearity: VIF 1.96-4.8 (Table 2). Partial correlations: η_max|K_eff = 0.35 vs K_eff|η_max = 0.12. Residualized K_eff: 3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap. Beta regression: η_max 10/12, K_eff 5/12 (Table 3).\n- 4.4 GAM: 6:6 split — targeted positive, shared-bias negative for K_eff. Acknowledged as \"suggestive.\"\n- 4.5 RF BFT Contrast: Table 4 — RF: K_eff p=0.03 (MMLU), 0.06 (ARC), R²≈0.70. Adaptive: K_eff mostly NS, R²≈0.22. GO/NO-GO: partial pass.\n- 4.6 Aggregation Gradient: Table 5 — threshold > majority > weighted > Bayesian for vulnerability. Ordinal ranking preserved across corrections.\n- 4.7 Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip: Table 6 — only 2/7 negative coefficients survive bootstrap (MMLU×PI and MMLU×auth).\n- 4.8 Practical Panel Design: min(η_max) dominates 12/12. K_eff IQR effect = 1.3pp. Family labels don't predict diversity.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** When diversity may help, practical implications, broader applicability.\n\n**Section 5.1 (Limitations):** Benchmark scope (MCQ only), attack coverage (6 types), panel size (K=3 only), capability-matched validation (r=0.70 confound), GAM validation (no bootstrap CIs).\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Restates main findings.\n\n**Appendix A:** Diversity-capability tradeoff visualization (K_eff vs η_max, log-linear fit, Pareto frontier, tier stratification).\n\n**Appendix B:** GO/NO-GO gate for BFT framing (Table 7).\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe full experiment design had 12 planned experiments. The paper covers:\n- plan_001 (15-model scale expansion): COMPLETED — core data, Tables 1-3\n- plan_003 (RF BFT contrast): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Table 4, borderline ARC\n- plan_007 (diversity-capability tradeoff): COMPLETED — Appendix A\n- plan_009 (VIF/multicollinearity): COMPLETED — Tables 2-3\n- plan_010 (aggregation gradient): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Tables 5-6, OLS gradient exists but clustered SE/bootstrap validation partially done\n\nNOT completed: plan_002 (attack expansion), plan_004 (capability-matched), plan_005 (two-stage algorithm benchmark), plan_006 (per-judge ASR concentration/Gini), plan_008 (panel size K=3,5,7,9), plan_011 (open-ended generation), plan_012 (partial-knowledge adversary).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Cover:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology**: Is the experimental design valid? Are the correction methods appropriate? Is the regression specification well-justified?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the claims supported by the evidence? Are there gaps in the statistical analysis?\n3. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? What critical experiments are missing?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the study?\n5. **Presentation quality**: Are the results clearly presented?\n\nProvide scores for Soundness, Excitement, Reproducibility (each 1-5), and an Overall Assessment (using the ARR scale above). List specific strengths and weaknesses. Be rigorous but fair.\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X (with label)\n\n### Confidence\nX/5\n```\n","uuid":"5aa7c164-e73d-494e-bbc4-8df7b20c7513","session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa"}
