{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01923XPUeAMP1sWbv2hqqNmK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\n3\tThe BFT analogy motivating multi-judge evaluation assumes non-adaptive faults: independent judges fail via uncorrelated events, and majority voting aggregates away errors. Our results show that under adaptive adversaries, panel vulnerability is dominated by the weakest individual judge ($\\eta_{\\max}$), not by panel diversity ($K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$). Across 15 models from 6 families, 455 panels, and 12 experimental conditions, $\\eta_{\\max}$ remains a robust predictor of attack success (significant in 5 to 8 of 12 conditions depending on non-independence correction; OLS: 11/12), while $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$'s significance collapses from 8/12 (OLS) to 0--1/12 after correction. The effect is directional but statistically fragile. Diversity and individual robustness are structurally correlated ($r{=}0.70$) in the current model ecosystem, further limiting causal interpretation of $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$'s independent effect.\n4\t\n5\tOur contributions operate at three levels. \\emph{Empirically}, $\\eta_{\\max}$ dominates $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ at a 5:1 ratio under bootstrap inference, establishing individual judge robustness as the primary predictor of panel security. The random-fault contrast reveals that $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$'s association with vulnerability is genuine under non-adaptive faults ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$, surviving all corrections) but inflated under adaptive attack (0--1/12 after correction). Adaptive adversaries introduce strategic variance that panel structure cannot predict. \\emph{Mechanistically}, GAM analysis reveals a clean 6:6 split by attack type: diversity is directionally harmful for targeted attacks and protective for shared-bias attacks, explaining the boundary that linear regression obscures. \\emph{Prescriptively}, aggregation rule choice fundamentally modulates vulnerability. Bayesian calibration reverses $K_{\\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (from 7+/1$-$ to 1+/6$-$) and weakens $\\eta_{\\max}$ (11/12 $\\to$ 9/12), and robustness-first panel selection ($\\min \\eta_{\\max}$) wins in all 12 conditions."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BFMmELrqhEp9QhcStqqn7H","session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"30071985-4218-4154-a4cc-a450732bfae2","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:58:31.891Z"}
