{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014eNSD6WFjstNxcEKTr9d2u","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPlan 003 Random-Fault Simulation Results\n2\t\n3\tServer path: /root/cert_manip_resist_eval/artifacts/results/results_plan003_random_fault.json\n4\t\n5\tSummary:\n6\t- Adaptive K_eff significant negative: 1/12 (arc_challenge×sycophancy)\n7\t- Random-fault K_eff significant negative: 0/12\n8\t- Random-fault R² range: 0.55-0.95\n9\t- Adaptive R² range: 0.14-0.73\n10\t\n11\tKey findings:\n12\t1. K_eff NOT significant even under random-fault (0/12) — structural collinearity with η_max\n13\t   - High K_eff panels include weak judges (llama-8b), so K_eff ↑ correlates with η_max ↑\n14\t   - Even controlling for η_max, 2nd/3rd weakest judge fault rates push up majority-vote failure\n15\t2. Random-fault R² much higher than adaptive R² — independent fault model is far more predictable\n16\t3. This means: adaptive attacks introduce variance that K_eff + η_max cannot explain\n17\t4. Reframing opportunity: instead of \"K_eff works for random but not adaptive\", \n18\t   the story is \"independent fault models (BFT assumption) predict panel failure well (high R²), \n19\t   but adaptive attacks break this predictability\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"e25e16f0-6330-42b5-9a4b-4616209ddc45","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:58:39.971Z"}
