{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABwuT4Hjh3iN76h6pHYsj6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPlan 010 Aggregation Analysis Results\n2\t\n3\tServer path: /root/cert_manip_resist_eval/artifacts/results/results_plan010_aggregation.json\n4\t\n5\tSummary:\n6\t| Method          | η_max sig | K_eff sig neg | mean R² |\n7\t|-----------------|-----------|---------------|---------|\n8\t| majority_vote   | 8/12      | 1/12          | 0.446   |\n9\t| weighted_vote   | 7/12      | 1/12          | 0.378   |\n10\t| threshold_vote  | 7/12      | 0/12          | 0.693   |\n11\t| bayesian        | 3/12      | 0/12          | 0.223   |\n12\t\n13\tKey findings:\n14\t1. Majority + weighted: η_max dominates, confirms weakest-link theorem\n15\t2. Threshold (unanimous): η_max still sig 7/12, but K_eff POSITIVELY sig in 7+ conditions — diversity harmful under unanimity (easier to break consensus)\n16\t3. Bayesian: η_max only 3/12 sig — reliability weighting effectively mitigates weakest-link by down-weighting unreliable judges\n17\t4. Conclusion: Weakest-link is NOT universal, depends on aggregation rule. Bayesian aggregation is a partial remedy."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"6264b520-b5fa-429a-8813-281505220514","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:58:41.164Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a401f18bbf9cfcde7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":52160,"tool_uses":27,"duration_ms":148190},"uuid":"42e384d5-d43c-48ab-857b-40d90ef505e2","session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1oPt8oD5UdbSVG2S3hKbY","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the full paper, methodology, data, and experimental results. Let me compose the review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper empirically decomposes multi-judge LLM panel vulnerability under adversarial attack into two structural predictors: weakest-judge vulnerability (eta_max) and panel diversity (K_eff). Using 15 models, 455 three-judge panels, 6 attack types, and 2 benchmarks, the authors find that eta_max dominates after non-independence correction (5:1 ratio under bootstrap), while K_eff's significance collapses. Additional analyses include a GAM-based attack taxonomy, aggregation rule comparison, and random-fault contrast. The paper makes a genuine methodological contribution around non-independence correction in combinatorial evaluation designs, though the experimental scope is narrower than the claims suggest.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Rigorous non-independence correction framework.** The paper's most compelling contribution is the systematic treatment of panel overlap. The 3-position max-p bootstrap procedure is well-motivated for the combinatorial design (each judge in 91/455 panels), and the transparency of reporting results across four correction methods (OLS, clustered SE, mixed effects, bootstrap) is exemplary. The ~2.5x SE inflation factor is a valuable practical finding for anyone doing combinatorial evaluation studies.\n\n- **S2: Clean experimental design for the core question.** The exhaustive C(15,3)=455 panel enumeration avoids sampling bias and enables a complete analysis of structural predictors. The log-log regression specification is well-justified (handling multiplicative effects and zero values), and the inclusion of dominance analysis, VIF diagnostics, and beta regression as robustness checks demonstrates statistical maturity.\n\n- **S3: The random-fault contrast is the paper's strongest causal argument.** Showing that K_eff is genuinely significant under random faults (surviving all corrections, R^2=0.73-0.81) but artifactual under adaptive attack (0-1/12) provides direct evidence for the mechanism: adaptive adversaries break the independence assumption underlying BFT. This is the closest the observational design comes to causal identification.\n\n- **S4: Aggregation gradient analysis reveals a non-obvious finding.** The K_eff direction reversal under Bayesian calibration (from 7+/1- to 1+/6-) is a genuinely novel observation that provides actionable design guidance. The vulnerability gradient from threshold (R^2=0.89) to Bayesian (R^2=0.104) is clearly presented.\n\n- **S5: Attack taxonomy via GAM.** The 6:6 split between targeted (diversity harmful) and shared-bias (diversity protective) attacks provides mechanistic explanatory power beyond the aggregate significance counts. This finding rescues the paper from being a purely negative result.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Severe capability confound undermines the central claim (Major).** The r=0.70 correlation between K_eff and eta_max is not merely a limitation to acknowledge -- it fundamentally compromises the causal interpretation the paper depends on. Six of the 15 models are AWQ INT4 quantized local models with severely degraded accuracy (qwen2.5-14b at 0.253 MMLU, llama3.1-8b at 0.320 ARC). These models are both the most diverse (cross-family) and the most vulnerable, creating a structural confound that no amount of regression correction can resolve observationally. The paper acknowledges this (\"observational regression cannot reliably disentangle diversity's causal contribution from the capability deficit\") but then proceeds to make strong directional claims (\"panel security is primarily an individual-judge quality problem\") that the confound does not support. A capability-matched experiment (plan_004 in the roadmap) was planned but never executed -- this is the single most important missing experiment.\n\n- **W2: Only K=3 panels tested (Major).** All 455 panels use exactly 3 judges. The generalizability to K=5, 7, or 9 (where BFT guarantees become more meaningful, since f < n/3 allows more faulty nodes) is untested. The planned panel-size scaling experiment (plan_008) was not completed. The paper's framing invokes BFT theory, which concerns panels large enough to tolerate multiple faults; K=3 is the degenerate case where a single compromised judge suffices for majority. The weakest-link dominance finding may be trivially true at K=3 but could reverse at larger K where diversity has more room to provide protection.\n\n- **W3: Narrow benchmark scope (Moderate).** Only MCQ benchmarks (MMLU 4-choice, ARC 2-choice) are used. Open-ended generation tasks (plan_011), where adversarial attacks and judge diversity operate differently, were planned but not executed. The paper claims to study \"LLM evaluation panels\" in general but the results apply only to constrained multiple-choice evaluation. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU 4/6 bootstrap eta_max vs ARC 1/6) already demonstrates sensitivity to task format, making the absence of a third, qualitatively different benchmark type a significant gap.\n\n- **W4: The \"adaptive adversary\" is not truly adaptive (Moderate).** Definition 2.1 defines an oracle adversary that selects the attack maximizing ASR. In practice, all 6 attacks are applied uniformly to all panels; the \"adaptive\" element is simply that the adversary uses the same attack against all judges in a panel and the per-condition analysis reveals which attacks work best. A truly adaptive adversary would select different attacks for different panels based on knowledge of panel composition -- i.e., implementing the arg max over attacks per panel. The gap between the formalization and the implementation weakens the theoretical contribution.\n\n- **W5: Aggregation gradient uses uncorrected OLS (Moderate).** The paper states that aggregation results \"are structurally independent of the non-independence concern\" because \"changing the aggregation rule alters the dependent variable, not the panel overlap structure.\" This reasoning is incorrect. The panel overlap structure generates correlated residuals regardless of the dependent variable; the same 455 panels share the same 15 judges under every aggregation rule. The aggregation gradient (Table 5) should be validated under the same correction methods applied to the main analysis. The raw summary file confirms the underlying data shows different patterns (e.g., Bayesian eta_max 3/12 vs. the paper's 9/12 under OLS, suggesting substantial inflation).\n\n- **W6: GAM taxonomy lacks statistical validation (Minor).** The 6:6 split is described as \"suggestive\" and lacks bootstrap confidence intervals on the GAM partial effects. The paper classifies position bias as \"targeted\" -- this is debatable since position bias is a shared cognitive bias across architectures, not a judge-specific vulnerability. Moving position bias to the shared-bias category would break the clean 6:6 into a 4:8 split. The taxonomy's robustness to reclassification is not tested.\n\n- **W7: K_eff computation details are underspecified (Minor).** Equation 1 for K_eff uses pairwise MI, but the text then says the actual computation goes through Gaussian copula correlation. The Miller-Madow bias correction is mentioned but the number of samples used to estimate MI (300 items per benchmark) may be insufficient for stable 3-category MI estimation, especially with the plug-in estimator. No sensitivity analysis on K_eff estimation stability is provided.\n\n- **W8: Local model quality raises floor effects (Minor).** Several quantized models have near-chance accuracy (qwen2.5-14b: 0.253/0.240, qwen2.5-32b: 0.373/0.450). Including models that are already failing on clean data conflates \"adversarial vulnerability\" with \"baseline incompetence.\" The sensitivity check excluding judges with clean accuracy below 0.5 is mentioned but results are not reported in the paper.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** The raw experiment data (plan010_aggregation_summary.txt) shows Bayesian eta_max at 3/12 significant, but Table 5 in the paper reports 9/12. Can you clarify this discrepancy? Are the Table 5 results OLS-only, and if so, why were corrections not applied given the known non-independence issue?\n\n- **Q2:** What happens to the main regression results if you restrict to models with clean accuracy above 0.5 (removing the 6 weakest quantized models)? You mention this sensitivity check was performed but do not report the result.\n\n- **Q3:** The K_eff formula (Eq. 1) computes a per-judge quantity averaged over K judges. For K=3, this is averaging over 3 terms each involving 2 pairwise MI values. How sensitive is K_eff to estimation noise in MI at n=300? Have you computed K_eff stability via bootstrap resampling of the 300 items?\n\n- **Q4:** Your \"adaptive\" adversary applies the same attack type uniformly across all panels. Have you considered implementing the true oracle adversary (selecting the best attack per panel) and testing whether the weakest-link dominance changes when the adversary is genuinely panel-adaptive?\n\n- **Q5:** For the random-fault contrast (Section 4.3), the simulation flips votes \"at rates matching observed per-judge error distributions.\" Does this mean each judge's random fault rate equals their observed clean error rate, or their adversarial vulnerability rate? The former would be circular; the latter would be the correct counterfactual.\n\n- **Q6:** The claim that the aggregation gradient is \"structurally independent\" of non-independence (Section 4.5) appears incorrect. Can you justify why correlated residuals from shared judges would not affect regression inference when only the DV changes?\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Reproducibility: 3/5\n- Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)\n\n### Confidence\n4/5\n\n---\n\n**Justification for Overall Assessment:** The paper addresses an important and timely question with a reasonable experimental framework. The non-independence correction methodology is a genuine contribution. However, the central claim (eta_max dominates, K_eff is fragile) rests on a confounded design where capability and diversity are structurally correlated at r=0.70, and the most critical deconfounding experiment (capability-matched panels) was not run. The K=3 restriction, MCQ-only benchmarks, and gap between the formalized adaptive adversary and the actual uniform attack application further limit the strength of the conclusions. The aggregation gradient, one of the more interesting findings, uses uncorrected inference despite known non-independence, and the raw data suggests the corrected results may differ substantially. These issues collectively place the paper in the Findings tier: the methodology and partial results are publishable, but the evidence does not yet support the strength of the claims for main conference acceptance."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a401f18bbf9cfcde7 (use SendMessage with to: 'a401f18bbf9cfcde7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 53886\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 148193</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e1da424-d161-46f8-8a38-0f3a6ee07afa","uuid":"1fd7cc5f-e882-425c-b78b-7572661f2041","timestamp":"2026-05-22T22:59:53.941Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main venue accept)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21%. Be calibrated: a score of 4 means top ~20%.\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Weakest Link, Not Diversity: Adversarial Vulnerability in Multi-Judge LLM Evaluation\"\n\nThe paper studies whether panel vulnerability in multi-judge LLM evaluation is driven by individual judge weakness (η_max) or structural diversity (K_eff). Using 15 models, 455 three-judge panels, 6 attack types, and 2 benchmarks (12 conditions), they find η_max dominates under non-independence correction (5-8/12 conditions significant) while K_eff collapses (0-3/12). They present methodological contributions around non-independence correction for combinatorial designs, GAM analysis showing a 6:6 targeted/shared-bias taxonomic split, RF vs adaptive BFT contrast, aggregation gradient analysis, and practical min(η_max) selection recommendation.\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages + appendices)\n\n**Section 1 (Intro):** Frames the problem as testing whether BFT diversity assumptions hold for LLM judge panels under adversarial attacks. 15 models, 6 families, 3 capability tiers, C(15,3)=455 panels, 6 attacks, 2 benchmarks. Three contributions claimed: (1) η_max dominance with benchmark-dependent strength, (2) methodological transparency on non-independence, (3) practical defense recommendations.\n\n**Section 2 (Related Work):** Four short paragraphs: LLM-as-Judge, Adversarial Attacks on LLM Judges, BFT, Panel Diversity in Evaluation. Very sparse — no specific citations beyond Zheng et al. 2023.\n\n**Section 3 (Method):**\n- 3.1: Panel construction — N=15, 6 families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta, Qwen, Mistral), 3 tiers (small/7B, medium/70B, API-frontier). η_max = max individual ASR. K_eff from eigenspectrum of MI matrix.\n- 3.2: 6 attacks in 2 categories: targeted (PI, SM, PB) and shared-bias (syco, VB, auth). 2 benchmarks (MMLU 4-choice, ARC 2-choice).\n- 3.3: Log-log OLS regression: log(ASR+ε) = β0 + β1·log(K_eff) + β2·log(η_max+ε) + ε\n- 3.4: Four correction methods for non-independence: naïve OLS, wild cluster bootstrap (B=9999, Rademacher, 3-position max-p), clustered SE (CR1, 15 clusters), mixed effects (random judge intercepts).\n\n**Section 4 (Experiments):**\n- 4.1 Main Results: Table 1 — OLS: η_max 11/12, K_eff 8/12; Bootstrap: 5/12, 1/12 (5:1 ratio); Clustered SE: 8/12, 0/12; Mixed effects: 7/12, 3/12. Benchmark asymmetry: MMLU 4/6 bootstrap η_max, ARC 1/6.\n- 4.2 SE Inflation: ~2.5× inflation, each judge in 91/455 panels.\n- 4.3 Multicollinearity: VIF 1.96-4.8 (Table 2). Partial correlations: η_max|K_eff = 0.35 vs K_eff|η_max = 0.12. Residualized K_eff: 3/12 OLS, 0/12 bootstrap. Beta regression: η_max 10/12, K_eff 5/12 (Table 3).\n- 4.4 GAM: 6:6 split — targeted positive, shared-bias negative for K_eff. Acknowledged as \"suggestive.\"\n- 4.5 RF BFT Contrast: Table 4 — RF: K_eff p=0.03 (MMLU), 0.06 (ARC), R²≈0.70. Adaptive: K_eff mostly NS, R²≈0.22. GO/NO-GO: partial pass.\n- 4.6 Aggregation Gradient: Table 5 — threshold > majority > weighted > Bayesian for vulnerability. Ordinal ranking preserved across corrections.\n- 4.7 Bayesian K_eff Sign Flip: Table 6 — only 2/7 negative coefficients survive bootstrap (MMLU×PI and MMLU×auth).\n- 4.8 Practical Panel Design: min(η_max) dominates 12/12. K_eff IQR effect = 1.3pp. Family labels don't predict diversity.\n\n**Section 5 (Discussion):** When diversity may help, practical implications, broader applicability.\n\n**Section 5.1 (Limitations):** Benchmark scope (MCQ only), attack coverage (6 types), panel size (K=3 only), capability-matched validation (r=0.70 confound), GAM validation (no bootstrap CIs).\n\n**Section 6 (Conclusion):** Restates main findings.\n\n**Appendix A:** Diversity-capability tradeoff visualization (K_eff vs η_max, log-linear fit, Pareto frontier, tier stratification).\n\n**Appendix B:** GO/NO-GO gate for BFT framing (Table 7).\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe full experiment design had 12 planned experiments. The paper covers:\n- plan_001 (15-model scale expansion): COMPLETED — core data, Tables 1-3\n- plan_003 (RF BFT contrast): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Table 4, borderline ARC\n- plan_007 (diversity-capability tradeoff): COMPLETED — Appendix A\n- plan_009 (VIF/multicollinearity): COMPLETED — Tables 2-3\n- plan_010 (aggregation gradient): PARTIALLY COMPLETED — Tables 5-6, OLS gradient exists but clustered SE/bootstrap validation partially done\n\nNOT completed: plan_002 (attack expansion), plan_004 (capability-matched), plan_005 (two-stage algorithm benchmark), plan_006 (per-judge ASR concentration/Gini), plan_008 (panel size K=3,5,7,9), plan_011 (open-ended generation), plan_012 (partial-knowledge adversary).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Cover:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology**: Is the experimental design valid? Are the correction methods appropriate? Is the regression specification well-justified?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the claims supported by the evidence? Are there gaps in the statistical analysis?\n3. **Experiment coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? What critical experiments are missing?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the study?\n5. **Presentation quality**: Are the results clearly presented?\n\nProvide scores for Soundness, Excitement, Reproducibility (each 1-5), and an Overall Assessment (using the ARR scale above). List specific strengths and weaknesses. Be rigorous but fair.\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X (with label)\n\n### Confidence\nX/5\n```\n","agentId":"a401f18bbf9cfcde7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the full paper, methodology, data, and experimental results. Let me compose the review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper empirically decomposes multi-judge LLM panel vulnerability under adversarial attack into two structural predictors: weakest-judge vulnerability (eta_max) and panel diversity (K_eff). Using 15 models, 455 three-judge panels, 6 attack types, and 2 benchmarks, the authors find that eta_max dominates after non-independence correction (5:1 ratio under bootstrap), while K_eff's significance collapses. Additional analyses include a GAM-based attack taxonomy, aggregation rule comparison, and random-fault contrast. The paper makes a genuine methodological contribution around non-independence correction in combinatorial evaluation designs, though the experimental scope is narrower than the claims suggest.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Rigorous non-independence correction framework.** The paper's most compelling contribution is the systematic treatment of panel overlap. The 3-position max-p bootstrap procedure is well-motivated for the combinatorial design (each judge in 91/455 panels), and the transparency of reporting results across four correction methods (OLS, clustered SE, mixed effects, bootstrap) is exemplary. The ~2.5x SE inflation factor is a valuable practical finding for anyone doing combinatorial evaluation studies.\n\n- **S2: Clean experimental design for the core question.** The exhaustive C(15,3)=455 panel enumeration avoids sampling bias and enables a complete analysis of structural predictors. The log-log regression specification is well-justified (handling multiplicative effects and zero values), and the inclusion of dominance analysis, VIF diagnostics, and beta regression as robustness checks demonstrates statistical maturity.\n\n- **S3: The random-fault contrast is the paper's strongest causal argument.** Showing that K_eff is genuinely significant under random faults (surviving all corrections, R^2=0.73-0.81) but artifactual under adaptive attack (0-1/12) provides direct evidence for the mechanism: adaptive adversaries break the independence assumption underlying BFT. This is the closest the observational design comes to causal identification.\n\n- **S4: Aggregation gradient analysis reveals a non-obvious finding.** The K_eff direction reversal under Bayesian calibration (from 7+/1- to 1+/6-) is a genuinely novel observation that provides actionable design guidance. The vulnerability gradient from threshold (R^2=0.89) to Bayesian (R^2=0.104) is clearly presented.\n\n- **S5: Attack taxonomy via GAM.** The 6:6 split between targeted (diversity harmful) and shared-bias (diversity protective) attacks provides mechanistic explanatory power beyond the aggregate significance counts. This finding rescues the paper from being a purely negative result.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Severe capability confound undermines the central claim (Major).** The r=0.70 correlation between K_eff and eta_max is not merely a limitation to acknowledge -- it fundamentally compromises the causal interpretation the paper depends on. Six of the 15 models are AWQ INT4 quantized local models with severely degraded accuracy (qwen2.5-14b at 0.253 MMLU, llama3.1-8b at 0.320 ARC). These models are both the most diverse (cross-family) and the most vulnerable, creating a structural confound that no amount of regression correction can resolve observationally. The paper acknowledges this (\"observational regression cannot reliably disentangle diversity's causal contribution from the capability deficit\") but then proceeds to make strong directional claims (\"panel security is primarily an individual-judge quality problem\") that the confound does not support. A capability-matched experiment (plan_004 in the roadmap) was planned but never executed -- this is the single most important missing experiment.\n\n- **W2: Only K=3 panels tested (Major).** All 455 panels use exactly 3 judges. The generalizability to K=5, 7, or 9 (where BFT guarantees become more meaningful, since f < n/3 allows more faulty nodes) is untested. The planned panel-size scaling experiment (plan_008) was not completed. The paper's framing invokes BFT theory, which concerns panels large enough to tolerate multiple faults; K=3 is the degenerate case where a single compromised judge suffices for majority. The weakest-link dominance finding may be trivially true at K=3 but could reverse at larger K where diversity has more room to provide protection.\n\n- **W3: Narrow benchmark scope (Moderate).** Only MCQ benchmarks (MMLU 4-choice, ARC 2-choice) are used. Open-ended generation tasks (plan_011), where adversarial attacks and judge diversity operate differently, were planned but not executed. The paper claims to study \"LLM evaluation panels\" in general but the results apply only to constrained multiple-choice evaluation. The benchmark asymmetry (MMLU 4/6 bootstrap eta_max vs ARC 1/6) already demonstrates sensitivity to task format, making the absence of a third, qualitatively different benchmark type a significant gap.\n\n- **W4: The \"adaptive adversary\" is not truly adaptive (Moderate).** Definition 2.1 defines an oracle adversary that selects the attack maximizing ASR. In practice, all 6 attacks are applied uniformly to all panels; the \"adaptive\" element is simply that the adversary uses the same attack against all judges in a panel and the per-condition analysis reveals which attacks work best. A truly adaptive adversary would select different attacks for different panels based on knowledge of panel composition -- i.e., implementing the arg max over attacks per panel. The gap between the formalization and the implementation weakens the theoretical contribution.\n\n- **W5: Aggregation gradient uses uncorrected OLS (Moderate).** The paper states that aggregation results \"are structurally independent of the non-independence concern\" because \"changing the aggregation rule alters the dependent variable, not the panel overlap structure.\" This reasoning is incorrect. The panel overlap structure generates correlated residuals regardless of the dependent variable; the same 455 panels share the same 15 judges under every aggregation rule. The aggregation gradient (Table 5) should be validated under the same correction methods applied to the main analysis. The raw summary file confirms the underlying data shows different patterns (e.g., Bayesian eta_max 3/12 vs. the paper's 9/12 under OLS, suggesting substantial inflation).\n\n- **W6: GAM taxonomy lacks statistical validation (Minor).** The 6:6 split is described as \"suggestive\" and lacks bootstrap confidence intervals on the GAM partial effects. The paper classifies position bias as \"targeted\" -- this is debatable since position bias is a shared cognitive bias across architectures, not a judge-specific vulnerability. Moving position bias to the shared-bias category would break the clean 6:6 into a 4:8 split. The taxonomy's robustness to reclassification is not tested.\n\n- **W7: K_eff computation details are underspecified (Minor).** Equation 1 for K_eff uses pairwise MI, but the text then says the actual computation goes through Gaussian copula correlation. The Miller-Madow bias correction is mentioned but the number of samples used to estimate MI (300 items per benchmark) may be insufficient for stable 3-category MI estimation, especially with the plug-in estimator. No sensitivity analysis on K_eff estimation stability is provided.\n\n- **W8: Local model quality raises floor effects (Minor).** Several quantized models have near-chance accuracy (qwen2.5-14b: 0.253/0.240, qwen2.5-32b: 0.373/0.450). Including models that are already failing on clean data conflates \"adversarial vulnerability\" with \"baseline incompetence.\" The sensitivity check excluding judges with clean accuracy below 0.5 is mentioned but results are not reported in the paper.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** The raw experiment data (plan010_aggregation_summary.txt) shows Bayesian eta_max at 3/12 significant, but Table 5 in the paper reports 9/12. Can you clarify this discrepancy? Are the Table 5 results OLS-only, and if so, why were corrections not applied given the known non-independence issue?\n\n- **Q2:** What happens to the main regression results if you restrict to models with clean accuracy above 0.5 (removing the 6 weakest quantized models)? You mention this sensitivity check was performed but do not report the result.\n\n- **Q3:** The K_eff formula (Eq. 1) computes a per-judge quantity averaged over K judges. For K=3, this is averaging over 3 terms each involving 2 pairwise MI values. How sensitive is K_eff to estimation noise in MI at n=300? Have you computed K_eff stability via bootstrap resampling of the 300 items?\n\n- **Q4:** Your \"adaptive\" adversary applies the same attack type uniformly across all panels. Have you considered implementing the true oracle adversary (selecting the best attack per panel) and testing whether the weakest-link dominance changes when the adversary is genuinely panel-adaptive?\n\n- **Q5:** For the random-fault contrast (Section 4.3), the simulation flips votes \"at rates matching observed per-judge error distributions.\" Does this mean each judge's random fault rate equals their observed clean error rate, or their adversarial vulnerability rate? The former would be circular; the latter would be the correct counterfactual.\n\n- **Q6:** The claim that the aggregation gradient is \"structurally independent\" of non-independence (Section 4.5) appears incorrect. Can you justify why correlated residuals from shared judges would not affect regression inference when only the DV changes?\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Reproducibility: 3/5\n- Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)\n\n### Confidence\n4/5\n\n---\n\n**Justification for Overall Assessment:** The paper addresses an important and timely question with a reasonable experimental framework. The non-independence correction methodology is a genuine contribution. However, the central claim (eta_max dominates, K_eff is fragile) rests on a confounded design where capability and diversity are structurally correlated at r=0.70, and the most critical deconfounding experiment (capability-matched panels) was not run. The K=3 restriction, MCQ-only benchmarks, and gap between the formalized adaptive adversary and the actual uniform attack application further limit the strength of the conclusions. The aggregation gradient, one of the more interesting findings, uses uncorrected inference despite known non-independence, and the raw data suggests the corrected results may differ substantially. These issues collectively place the paper in the Findings tier: the methodology and partial results are publishable, but the evidence does not yet support the strength of the claims for main conference acceptance."}],"totalDurationMs":148193,"totalTokens":53886,"totalToolUseCount":27,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1371,"cache_read_input_tokens":49959,"output_tokens":2555,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1371},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2555,"cache_read_input_tokens":49959,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1371,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":17,"searchCount":7,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
